// StudyHub — textbook points beyond the main notes, rewritten in simple language (build-authored.js)
const SUPPLEMENT_FINAL_DATA = [{"id":"sup-geo-ch7-01","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Aryabhata: India's early geographer","content":"**Who:** Aryabhata (476–550 CE), a great Indian astronomer, mathematician and scientist.\n• Credited with the idea of **zero**, an approximate value of **π**, and early **trigonometry**.\n\n**Geography in his work**\n• The Sanskrit word for geography is **Bhugol shastra** (*bhu* = earth, *gol* = round) — the Earth was known to be **round**.\n• In the *Surya Siddhanta* (about 500 CE) he gave knowledge of the **spherical Earth**, eclipses and geological time.\n• He said the Earth **rotates on its axis**, causing day and night.\n• He estimated the Earth's circumference at **39,968 km** — only **0.27% off** today's value, using no accurate instruments (he used two places on the same latitude and the time difference measured by the moon's position or a water clock).\n• He believed **Ujjain** lay on the **Tropic of Cancer**.\n\n**Honour:** India's first artificial satellite (**1975**) was named **Aryabhata**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":0},{"id":"sup-geo-ch1-02","topicId":"geo-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ James Rennell and the Map of Hindoostan","content":"**James Rennell (1742–1830)** — English geographer and oceanographer.\n• Learned surveying in the Royal Navy (1756–63); joined the **East India Company** in 1763.\n• **Robert Clive** made him **Surveyor General of Bengal (1767–77)** — the Company needed accurate maps to control a large territory.\n\n**Work**\n• Began surveying Bengal in **1764**; published *The Bengal Atlas* (**1779**) — the most accurate maps for the next 60 years.\n• Carried out the **first comprehensive geographical survey of India**.\n• **Method:** collect earlier maps, measure road distances, fix coordinates of control points, then draw a **graticule (grid)**.\n• **Map of Hindoostan (1782)** — \"the first approximately correct map of India\".\n• The map's cartouche shows Britain as a lion and Britannia on a pedestal of victories; **red** was first used by him to mark British territory.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":1},{"id":"sup-geo-ch1-03","topicId":"geo-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Who drew the first contour lines? Charles Hutton","content":"• **Charles Hutton**, a British mathematician, used contour lines for the first time in a survey of the Scottish peak **Schiehallion in 1774**.\n• His original aim was not to show height — he wanted to **weigh the mountain**!\n• His charts and survey tables survived, though the map itself did not.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":2},{"id":"sup-geo-ch1-04","topicId":"geo-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Quick facts: scale and contour lines","content":"• The **one-inch sheet** has scale **1 : 50,000** (2 cm = 1 km).\n\n**Features of contour lines**\n• Drawn at a **fixed vertical interval** (e.g. 20 m) which depends on map scale and relief.\n• Contour lines **never cross** each other; they may touch.\n• Every **fifth line** is drawn thick — the **contour index**.\n\n**Reading the slope**\n| Spacing | Slope |\n| --- | --- |\n| Close together | **steep** |\n| Far apart | **gentle** |\n| Equal intervals | **uniform** |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":3},{"id":"sup-geo-ch1-05","topicId":"geo-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Reading a map extract (Banas River area)","content":"**National Atlas:** the National Atlas Organisation was created in **1956**; the first Hindi atlas *Bharat: Rashtriya Atlas* was published in **1957**.\n\n**Worked reading of a topo-sheet extract**\n• **Drainage:** the **Banas River** crosses the map; **Balaram Nadi** is its tributary; they meet at a **confluence** (grid 4292). Arrows show flow direction.\n• **Streams:** **blue** = streams with water; **black** = dry streams.\n• **Relief:** contours show steep slope/escarpment, a **saddle**, a **ridge/watershed**; **spot height 542** marks a peak.\n• **Land use:** **green** = forest (dense jungle, mixed jungle, fairly dense jungle); **white** = barren land or open scrub; a white patch in the forest shows **deforestation**.\n• **Settlements:** nucleated (compact), scattered and linear; shown by **red squares**; single squares = single huts.\n• **Transport:** cart tracks connect settlements; broken red lines are **pack tracks**.\n\n**Values:** the **Survey of India** is the oldest scientific organisation of India — surveyors work in tough, uninhabited terrain.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":4},{"id":"sup-geo-ch5-06","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Flood safety measures","content":"**What governments should do**\n• Maintain **dykes and embankments**.\n• Provide **flood shelters** in vulnerable places so people and livestock can be evacuated.\n• Station **rescue equipment** (boats, communication lines) near flood-prone areas.\n• Keep **dry food, clean water and medicines** ready to stop diseases.\n\n**What people should do**\n• Know the terrain and the nearest safe places.\n• Keep a **long stick** to wade through water; **switch off mains** to avoid short-circuits.\n• Follow **TV/radio warnings**; place valuables at a height; block toilets with sandbags to stop sewage backflow.\n• Beware of **snakes**; store food, water and medicines.\n\n**Did you know?** Himachal Pradesh was devastated by floods in **July 2023** (over 100 deaths, losses above ₹3,700 crore; 20 NDRF teams were deployed). Delhi also flooded in mid-2023.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":5},{"id":"sup-geo-ch5-07","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Role of the people in a disaster","content":"The **first people to cope** with a disaster are usually the victims themselves.\n• Every house should have an **emergency first-aid kit**, and some members should know **basic first aid**.\n• Awareness reduces damage — a hotel obeying rules on **emergency exits and fire extinguishers** saves lives.\n• People in war zones can build **underground bunkers**; coastal people can plant **trees or build tsunami walls**.\n• Everyone should be **alert to suspicious elements** that may cause man-made disasters.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":6},{"id":"sup-geo-ch5-08","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Case study: the Deepwater Horizon oil spill","content":"• **April 2010:** the offshore rig *Deepwater Horizon* (owned by **BP**) in the **Gulf of Mexico** exploded after a blowout — **11 workers died, 16 were injured**.\n• About **4.9 million barrels** of oil spilled — the **largest oil spill in petroleum history**; it was contained in **September 2010**.\n• **Effects:** severe damage to marine life, fishing and tourism; effects still seen in 2013 (dolphins dying, oil on distant beaches).\n• **BP was found guilty** of cost-cutting and inadequate safety systems.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":7},{"id":"sup-geo-ch6-09","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Equatorial region: vegetation and wildlife","content":"**Vegetation**\n• Dense **hardwood forests** — mahogany, ebony, rosewood, cinchona, coconut, rubber.\n• Lianas, creepers, tall grass, ferns and bamboo grow between trees; the **canopy** is so thick that the forest floor is dark.\n• Slightly different from the Amazon and Zaire rainforests because of **nearness to the sea**.\n\n**Wildlife**\n• **Arboreal** animals: monkeys, apes, colourful birds.\n• Lizards, snakes, crocodiles and insects on swampy floors.\n• Elephants and wild cats stay on the **outskirts** (the interior is too dense).","source":"ocr_supplement","order":8},{"id":"sup-geo-ch7-10","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Wildlife sanctuaries of India","content":"| Sanctuary | State | Known for |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Kachchh Desert** | Gujarat | saline desert, \"Flamingo City\", resident and migratory birds, Houbara bustard |\n| **Wild Ass Sanctuary** (Little Rann of Kachchh) | Gujarat | wild ass |\n| **Anamalai (Indira Gandhi)** | Tamil Nadu (Western Ghats) | gaur, panther, elephant, sloth bear, crocodiles in Amaravati reservoir |\n| **Chilika** | Odisha | migratory birds: flamingo, pelican, spoonbill, sea eagle, ibis… |\n| **Sonai Rupai** | Assam | Indian bison, elephant, hornbills, pelicans |\n| **Bhimbandh** | Bihar | hot-water springs, resident and winter-migrant birds |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":9},{"id":"sup-geo-ch7-11","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ National parks of India","content":"| National park | Location | Known for |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Ranthambore** | Rajasthan | tiger protection; banyan, dhok, mango trees |\n| **Sundarbans** | West Bengal | major tiger reserve |\n| **Bandhavgarh** | Madhya Pradesh | tigers |\n| **Kanha** | Madhya Pradesh | best-maintained park; inspired Kipling's *The Jungle Book* |\n| **Corbett** | Uttarakhand (Himalayan foothills) | tigers; named after Jim Corbett |\n| **Gir** | Gujarat | **Asiatic lion** |\n| **Keoladeo Ghana** | Bharatpur, Rajasthan | formerly Bharatpur Bird Sanctuary |\n| **Periyar** | Kerala | elephants and tigers |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":10},{"id":"sup-civ-ch1-12","topicId":"civ-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Nyaya Panchayats: justice at the doorstep","content":"A proposal (Government of India press release, 26 August 2011) to set up **Nyaya Panchayats** — village-level courts.\n\n• One in every **Gram Panchayat** or cluster of Gram Panchayats.\n• Aim: **fair and speedy justice**, civil and criminal, at people's doorsteps, outside the formal court system.\n• **Nyaya Panchas** are **elected** by the people of the area; seats are reserved for **women, Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes**.\n• **Conciliation** (settlement through mutual agreement) is the main method of resolving disputes.\n• **Nyaya Sahayaks** assist the Nyaya Panchayat.\n• They are meant to be **separate and independent** of the Gram Panchayats.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":11},{"id":"sup-civ-ch1-13","topicId":"civ-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Key words: Organs of the Indian Government","content":"| Term | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Coalition government** | a government formed by several parties working together |\n| **Electoral college** | the body that elects the President and Vice President |\n| **Ex-officio** | by virtue of the office held |\n| **Fallible** | capable of making mistakes |\n| **Ordinance** | a temporary law issued by the President when Parliament is not in session; valid for six months |\n| **Parliamentary democracy** | people elect the government |\n| **Precedent** | an earlier court decision that guides similar cases |\n| **Proclamation of Emergency** | declared by the President if the nation's security is threatened by war, external aggression or armed rebellion; Fundamental Rights are suspended |\n| **Proportional representation** | seats in the legislature match the share of votes |\n| **Secret ballot** | the voter's choice is kept confidential |\n| **Single transferable vote** | a proportional-representation system in which voters rank candidates in order of preference |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":12},{"id":"sup-civ-ch2-14","topicId":"civ-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Functions of UN agencies (UNESCO, UNICEF, WHO, ILO)","content":"| Agency | Main functions |\n| --- | --- |\n| **UNESCO** | promotes education for children and adults; finances basic research in developing countries; organises conferences for cultural cooperation; helps preserve cultural heritage |\n| **UNICEF** | improves the quality of life of children (health, education); helps nations run child-care centres; gives medicines and assistance to young mothers and babies; fights child labour and exploitation |\n| **WHO** | runs worldwide campaigns against diseases like AIDS, cholera, plague, malaria and COVID-19; promotes research and awareness |\n| **ILO** | ensures social and economic justice for workers; promotes social security; spreads awareness of workers' rights |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":13},{"id":"sup-bio-ch4-15","topicId":"bio-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ The atmosphere and its layers","content":"The **atmosphere** is the envelope of air around the Earth. It has distinct layers:\n\n| Layer | Height (approx.) | Density | Importance |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Troposphere** | up to about 12 km | highest | holds most of the air; weather and clouds occur here |\n| **Stratosphere** | 10 – 50 km | less than troposphere | has the **ozone layer**, which absorbs most UV rays (UV causes cataract and cancer) |\n| **Mesosphere** | 50 – 80 km | low | burns up meteors (rocky masses from space) |\n| **Thermosphere (ionosphere)** | 80 – 700 km | extremely low | ionised gases; **reflects radio waves** for long-distance communication; most satellites orbit here |\n| **Exosphere** | 700 – 10,000 km | very thin | hydrogen and helium; no air to breathe |\n\n**Why it matters:** the atmosphere is a **protective blanket** — it absorbs harmful UV radiation, reflects excess heat back into space (dust, water vapour, clouds), controls climate and lets life survive.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":14},{"id":"sup2-ch2-measurement-001","topicId":"ch2-measurement","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Archimedes and the \"Eureka!\" moment","content":"**Who:** Archimedes, a Greek scientist, famous for **Archimedes' principle**.\n\n**The story:** He stepped into a bath tub that was full to the brim and saw some water **overflow**. That gave him the clue — a body dipped in water pushes out (displaces) water.\n• He ran through the streets shouting **\"Eureka!\"** — \"I have got it!\"\n• He used the idea to find how much **impurity (silver) was mixed with gold** in the royal crown.\n\n**Why it matters:** The same idea explains why objects float or sink and is the basis of the **principle of floatation**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":15},{"id":"sup2-ch2-measurement-002","topicId":"ch2-measurement","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Lactometer: checking the purity of milk","content":"A **lactometer** is a device that measures the **relative density** of milk to find out whether water has been mixed in (adulteration).\n\n**Three parts:**\n• **Floating bulb** — makes it float.\n• **Gravity bulb** — holds mercury or lead shots so the stem stays upright.\n• **Stem** — graduated (marked) from **1.000 to 1.045**.\n\n**Why 1.000–1.045?** Pure water has relative density **1.000**, pure milk about **1.045**.\n\n**How to use:** Dip the bulb in the milk and read the mark on the stem. A reading **lower than 1.045** means the milk has been diluted with water.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":16},{"id":"sup2-ch3-force-003","topicId":"ch3-force","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Moment of a force: the hand flour grinder","content":"The hand-operated **flour grinder** (chakki) of village homes has its **handle fixed near the rim**.\n\n• The turning effect (moment) of a force = force × **perpendicular distance** from the axis of rotation.\n• A handle near the rim makes this distance **maximum**.\n• So the stone can be turned easily with a **small force**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":17},{"id":"sup2-ch3-force-004","topicId":"ch3-force","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Pressure in daily life: why coolies wear turbans","content":"Pressure is given by $P = \\frac{F}{A}$ — for the same force $F$, a **larger area $A$ gives less pressure**, and a smaller area gives more.\n\n**Example:** Coolies wear a **turban (a pad of cloth)** on the head while carrying heavy loads.\n• It increases the **area of contact**.\n• The pressure of the load is spread out, so it is **comfortable** and strains the head less.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":18},{"id":"sup2-ch3-force-005","topicId":"ch3-force","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Work: why a rickshaw puller does no work against weight","content":"Work is done only when a force **moves the body in the direction of the force**.\n\n**Example:** A rickshaw puller pulls a rickshaw along a level road.\n• The **weight** of the passengers acts **vertically downwards**.\n• The rickshaw moves **horizontally**.\n• So **no work** is done against the weight (force and displacement are at right angles).","source":"ocr_supplement","order":19},{"id":"sup2-ch4-energy-006","topicId":"ch4-energy","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Kinetic energy: examples","content":"**Kinetic energy** is the energy a body has because it is **moving**.\n\n**Examples:**\n• A strong **wind** can uproot big trees; wind energy also runs **windmills**.\n• A fast-moving **bullet** can pierce thick metal sheets.\n• Flowing water, a running car or train, a speeding fan, a flying bird and an orbiting satellite all have kinetic energy.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":20},{"id":"sup2-ch4-energy-007","topicId":"ch4-energy","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Hydroelectric power: energy conversion","content":"**Hydroelectric power station — step by step**\n\n1. Water stored behind a dam has **gravitational potential energy**.\n2. When the gates open, the water falls and the potential energy changes to **kinetic energy**.\n3. The fast water turns the **turbine** of a generator.\n4. The generator changes the kinetic energy into **electrical energy**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":21},{"id":"sup2-ch4-energy-009","topicId":"ch4-energy","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Conservation of mechanical energy","content":"Potential energy can change into kinetic energy and back again.\n\n**Law of conservation of mechanical energy:** if there is **no friction**, the sum of **kinetic + potential energy** of an object (or system) stays **constant**.\n\nYou can see it in a swinging pendulum, a falling ball, a roller-coaster and many everyday situations.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":22},{"id":"sup2-ch1-matter-011","topicId":"ch1-matter","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Heat and temperature (recap)","content":"• **Heat** is a form of energy that gives the sensation of warmth or coldness.\n• **Temperature** measures the degree of hotness or coldness of a body.\n• Heat always flows from a **hotter** body to a **colder** body, and the flow continues until both are at the **same temperature**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":23},{"id":"sup2-ch6-heat-012","topicId":"ch6-heat","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Thermal expansion at work: iron rim on a cart wheel","content":"**Problem:** the iron rim of a bullock-cart or tonga wheel must fit the wooden wheel very tightly.\n\n**Method:**\n1. The rim is made **slightly smaller** than the wheel.\n2. It is **heated**, so it expands and slips over the wheel.\n3. **Cold water** is poured on it — it contracts and **grips** the wheel tightly.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":24},{"id":"sup2-ch7-sound-013","topicId":"ch7-sound","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Pitch and frequency in wind instruments (flute)","content":"In wind instruments — flute, clarinet, shehnai, whistle — a column of air inside a pipe is made to vibrate by blowing.\n\n**Rule:** the **shorter** the vibrating air column, the **higher** the frequency and the **shriller** (higher-pitched) the sound.\n\n**In a flute:**\n• All holes closed → air column is longest → **lowest frequency**, flat sound.\n• Opening a hole shortens the air column → frequency **increases**, sound becomes shriller.\n• The player changes the pitch by opening and closing different holes.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":25},{"id":"sup2-ch7-sound-014","topicId":"ch7-sound","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Tuning fork and the Indian scale","content":"**Tuning fork:** a U-shaped metal piece with a **stem** and two **prongs**.\n• Hit one prong gently with a rubber pad — both prongs vibrate back and forth and produce a pure sound.\n\n**Indian music:** the classical scale (Sargam) has eight notes — **sa, re, ga, ma, pa, dha, ni, sa**. Tuning forks correspond to these notes; the **keynote (lowest) is taken as 256 Hz**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":26},{"id":"sup2-ch7-sound-015","topicId":"ch7-sound","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Loudness levels in decibels (dB)","content":"Loudness is measured in **decibels (dB)**.\n\n| Source of sound | Loudness (dB) | Feeling |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Rustling of leaves | 10 | very, very faint |\n| Whisper | 20 – 25 | very faint |\n| Ordinary conversation | 40 – 60 | normal |\n| Telephone bell | 70 | loud |\n| Heavy city traffic | 80 – 90 | very loud |\n| Factory noise | 100 – 110 | painfully loud |\n| Rock concert | 110 – 120 | painfully loud |\n| Jet plane | 130 – 140 | very painfully loud |\n| Rocket at take-off | 200 | dangerously loud |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":27},{"id":"sup2-ch8-electricity-016","topicId":"ch8-electricity","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Potential difference, resistor and resistance","content":"• The SI unit of **potential difference** is the **volt (V)**, named after **Alessandro Volta**, who made the first electric cell.\n• **Resistor:** any part of a circuit that **opposes (restricts) the flow of current** — bulb, tube light, CFL, fan, heater, geyser, mixer-grinder.\n• **Resistance:** the opposition a resistor offers to current. It is denoted by **R**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":28},{"id":"sup2-ch8-electricity-017","topicId":"ch8-electricity","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Power and voltage rating of common appliances","content":"All household appliances are made for the **220 V** supply. Typical power ratings:\n\n| Appliance | Power (W) |\n| --- | --- |\n| LED bulb | 1 – 10 |\n| CFL | 5 – 18 |\n| Tube light (4 ft) | 36 – 40 |\n| Electric fan | 60 – 100 |\n| Electric bulb | 15 – 500 |\n| Refrigerator (165 L) | 150 |\n| Colour TV | 120 – 150 |\n| Desert cooler | 100 – 150 |\n| Electric iron | 450 – 1200 |\n| Mixer-grinder | 400 – 1200 |\n| Room heater | 1000 – 1500 |\n| Geyser | 1500 – 2500 |\n| Air conditioner (1.5 t) | 1800 – 2500 |\n| Electric oven | 2000 – 3000 |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":29},{"id":"sup2-ch8-electricity-018","topicId":"ch8-electricity","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ From the electric meter to the rooms","content":"1. Supply enters through the **electric meter**.\n2. It then passes through the **main fuse** and the **main switch**.\n3. From the main switch, **insulated copper wires** carry current to the rooms.\n4. The wires connect to bulbs, fans, tube lights and other appliances.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":30},{"id":"sup2-ch8-electricity-019","topicId":"ch8-electricity","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Electrical hazard: faulty circuits","content":"**Defective circuits** — loose joints, poor insulation of wires, faulty switches, sockets or plugs — can cause **sparking**, which may start a fire.\n\n**Safety:** use good insulated wires, fit proper fuses and get faulty fittings repaired.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":31},{"id":"sup2-ch8-electricity-020","topicId":"ch8-electricity","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Charged and uncharged bodies: charging by friction","content":"• A body with electric charge is **charged**; one without is **uncharged** — most bodies are normally uncharged.\n• Rub materials such as **glass, ebonite, amber, plastic or rubber** with **silk, wool, fur or flannel** and they become **charged (electrified)**.\n• This is **charging by friction**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":32},{"id":"sup2-ch8-electricity-021","topicId":"ch8-electricity","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Franklin's kite experiment","content":"**Aim:** to show that lightning is electricity.\n\n**Set-up:** a silk kite with a 30 cm iron wire as the central spar, tied to a silk thread whose other end carried an iron key.\n\n**Observations:**\n• Flown in a thunderstorm — **nothing** happened at first.\n• After it started **raining**, the wet silk thread conducted charge; **sparks** jumped from the key to his knuckles.\n• The charge flowed from the **clouds → key → his body → earth**.\n\n**Conclusion:** thunder clouds carry electric charge — lightning is a huge electric spark. (Franklin was lucky not to be electrocuted — never try this!)","source":"ocr_supplement","order":33},{"id":"sup2-ch8-electricity-022","topicId":"ch8-electricity","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Lightning conductor","content":"A **lightning conductor** is a metal rod with pointed spikes fixed on top of a tall building and joined to the earth by a thick copper strip.\n\n• The spikes build up **induced positive charge** that **neutralises the negative charge on the clouds**, so lightning is less likely.\n• If lightning does strike, the charge is carried safely **to the earth** through the conductor and the building is protected.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":34},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch3-024","topicId":"chem-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Separating two liquids by distillation","content":"**Used for:** a mixture of two **miscible liquids** with different boiling points.\n\n**Steps:**\n1. Heat the mixture in a **distilling flask**.\n2. The liquid with the **lower boiling point** turns to vapour first; it is cooled in the condenser and collected in a receiver.\n3. The **temperature stays constant** until all of that liquid has distilled over.\n4. Heating continues, the temperature rises, and the **second liquid** vaporises at its own boiling point and is collected in another receiver.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":35},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch4-025","topicId":"chem-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Early ideas of the atom: Kanada and Democritus","content":"**Maharishi Kanada** (about 600 BC, India)\n• Matter is made of **indestructible particles called paramanus** (*param* = ultimate, *anu* = particle) — what we now call **atoms**.\n• A paramanu does not exist alone; paramanus combine to form a bigger particle called **anu** — now known as a **molecule**.\n\n**Democritus** (460–370 BC, Greece)\n• Called the paramanu an **atom**, from the Greek *atomos* meaning **indivisible**.\n• Atoms combine chemically to form molecules.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":36},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch5-026","topicId":"chem-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Writing the symbol with mass number and atomic number","content":"For an element X with **mass number A** and **atomic number Z**:\n\n$$ {}^{A}_{Z}X $$\n\n• **A** is written as a **superscript** (top left).\n• **Z** is written as a **subscript** (bottom left).\n\nExample: $ {}^{23}_{11}\\text{Na} $ — mass number 23, atomic number 11.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":37},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch4-027","topicId":"chem-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Dalton's atomic theory — what still holds","content":"Several features of Dalton's theory were **contradicted** by later discoveries (atoms are divisible; atoms of one element can differ, as in isotopes).\n\n**But its essence is still used today:** matter is made of atoms and atoms combine in simple whole-number ratios to form compounds.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":38},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch5-028","topicId":"chem-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Variable valency: phosphorus","content":"Some elements show **more than one valency**.\n\n**Phosphorus** has valencies **3 and 5**:\n• $\\mathrm{PCl}_3$ (phosphorus **tri**chloride) — three chlorine atoms → valency **3** (lower).\n• $\\mathrm{PCl}_5$ (phosphorus **penta**chloride) — five chlorine atoms → valency **5** (higher).","source":"ocr_supplement","order":39},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch5-029","topicId":"chem-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Growth in the number of known elements","content":"• By the beginning of the **nineteenth century**, **26** elements were known.\n• By the **twentieth century** the number had risen to **81**.\n• Today **118** elements are known.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":40},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch5-030","topicId":"chem-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Symbols from Latin and Greek names","content":"Some symbols come from the **Latin or Greek** name of the element.\n\n| Element | Latin / Greek name | Symbol |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Sodium | Natrium | Na |\n| Potassium | Kalium | K |\n| Iron | Ferrum | Fe |\n| Copper | Cuprum | Cu |\n| Silver | Argentum | Ag |\n| Gold | Aurum | Au |\n| Mercury | Hydrargyrum | Hg |\n| Lead | Plumbum | Pb |\n| Tin | Stannum | Sn |\n| Antimony | Stibium | Sb |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":41},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch5-031","topicId":"chem-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Modern concept of valency: lower and higher states","content":"Some metals show **two valency states**, written with a Roman numeral in brackets.\n\n| Element | Lower valency | Higher valency |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Copper (Cu) | $\\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$ — Cu(I), **cuprous** | $\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ — Cu(II), **cupric** |\n| Iron (Fe) | $\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ — Fe(II), **ferrous** | $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ — Fe(III), **ferric** |\n| Silver (Ag) | $\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}$ — Ag(I), **argentous** | $\\mathrm{Ag}^{2+}$ — Ag(II), **argentic** |\n| Lead (Pb) | $\\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ — Pb(II), **plumbous** | $\\mathrm{Pb}^{4+}$ — Pb(IV), **plumbic** |\n| Tin (Sn) | $\\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}$ — Sn(II), **stannous** | $\\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}$ — Sn(IV), **stannic** |\n| Mercury (Hg) | Hg(I), **mercurous** | Hg(II), **mercuric** |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":42},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch5-032","topicId":"chem-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Table of common radicals (ions)","content":"**Positive radicals (cations)**\n\n| Valency | Radicals |\n| --- | --- |\n| **1+** | Sodium $\\mathrm{Na}^{+}$, Potassium $\\mathrm{K}^{+}$, Copper(I)/Cuprous $\\mathrm{Cu}^{+}$, Silver $\\mathrm{Ag}^{+}$, Mercury(I) $\\mathrm{Hg}^{+}$, Hydrogen $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$, Ammonium $\\mathrm{NH}_4^{+}$ |\n| **2+** | Magnesium $\\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$, Calcium $\\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$, Barium $\\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}$, Zinc $\\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$, Nickel $\\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$, Copper(II)/Cupric $\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$, Iron(II)/Ferrous $\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$, Lead(II) $\\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$, Tin(II) $\\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}$, Mercury(II) $\\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$, Manganese(II) $\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ |\n| **3+** | Aluminium $\\mathrm{Al}^{3+}$, Iron(III)/Ferric $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$, Chromium(III) $\\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ |\n| **4+** | Tin(IV)/Stannic $\\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}$, Platinum(IV) $\\mathrm{Pt}^{4+}$ |\n\n**Negative radicals (anions)**\n\n| Valency | Radicals |\n| --- | --- |\n| **1−** | Chloride $\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$, Bromide $\\mathrm{Br}^{-}$, Iodide $\\mathrm{I}^{-}$, Hydroxide $\\mathrm{OH}^{-}$, Nitrite $\\mathrm{NO}_2^{-}$, Nitrate $\\mathrm{NO}_3^{-}$, Bicarbonate $\\mathrm{HCO}_3^{-}$, Bisulphite $\\mathrm{HSO}_3^{-}$, Bisulphate $\\mathrm{HSO}_4^{-}$, Acetate $\\mathrm{CH}_3\\mathrm{COO}^{-}$, Permanganate $\\mathrm{MnO}_4^{-}$ |\n| **2−** | Oxide $\\mathrm{O}^{2-}$, Sulphide $\\mathrm{S}^{2-}$, Sulphite $\\mathrm{SO}_3^{2-}$, Sulphate $\\mathrm{SO}_4^{2-}$, Carbonate $\\mathrm{CO}_3^{2-}$, Chromate $\\mathrm{CrO}_4^{2-}$, Dichromate $\\mathrm{Cr}_2\\mathrm{O}_7^{2-}$, Manganate $\\mathrm{MnO}_4^{2-}$ |\n| **3−** | Nitride $\\mathrm{N}^{3-}$, Phosphate $\\mathrm{PO}_4^{3-}$ |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":43},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch5-033","topicId":"chem-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Why balanced equations are useful","content":"A **balanced chemical equation** is a short way of showing a lot of information:\n• which substances react (**reactants**) and which form (**products**);\n• the **number of atoms/molecules** of each;\n• that **mass is conserved** — the same number of each kind of atom on both sides.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":44},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch6-034","topicId":"chem-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Acidity: too much acid in the stomach","content":"The stomach secretes **hydrochloric acid** to help digest food.\n\nIf **too much acid** is secreted — for example, when we **overeat** or stay **hungry for a long time** — it causes **acidity and indigestion**. Antacids (mild bases) give relief by neutralising the extra acid.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":45},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch7-035","topicId":"chem-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Laboratory preparation of hydrogen: apparatus","content":"**Reaction:** zinc + dilute sulphuric (or hydrochloric) acid → hydrogen.\n\n**Apparatus and procedure**\n1. Put a few pieces of **granulated zinc** in a round-bottom flask.\n2. Close the mouth with an **airtight, two-holed cork**.\n3. Fit a **thistle funnel** through one hole (to add acid) and a **delivery tube** through the other.\n4. Place the free end of the delivery tube under a **beehive shelf** in a trough of water.\n5. Collect the hydrogen by **downward displacement of water** (it is almost insoluble in water).","source":"ocr_supplement","order":46},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch8-036","topicId":"chem-ch8","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Water: the universal solvent","content":"Water dissolves more substances than any other liquid, so it is called the **universal solvent**.\n\n• It can even dissolve tiny particles from the **container** in which it is kept.\n• **Very pure (distilled) water** is not good to drink for long — it can even attack tissues inside the body because it dissolves salts so readily.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":47},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch8-037","topicId":"chem-ch8","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Removal of hardness of water","content":"**Hard water** contains dissolved salts of **calcium and magnesium**. It does not form lather easily with soap.\n\n• Removing these salts is called **softening** of water.\n• Soft water is needed in homes, laundries and factories.\n• Methods include boiling (for temporary hardness), adding washing soda, the permutit method and ion-exchange.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":48},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch9-038","topicId":"chem-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Organic compounds around us","content":"Organic compounds can be **natural** or **man-made**.\n\n• **Natural:** starch, wax, vinegar, alcohol, silk, wool, cotton.\n• **Man-made:** dyes, detergents, soaps, plastics, nylon, polyester, paper, polythene, perfumes, disinfectants and many medicines.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":49},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch9-039","topicId":"chem-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Diamond: occurrence","content":"• Diamond is the **purest naturally occurring form of carbon**.\n• Found in South Africa, Brazil, Namibia, Russia, Australia, the U.S.A. and India.\n• In India: **Golconda (Karnataka)** and **Panna (Madhya Pradesh)**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":50},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch9-040","topicId":"chem-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Amorphous substances","content":"**Amorphous** means \"without definite shape\".\n\n• Their atoms or molecules are **not arranged in any regular pattern**.\n• Examples: anhydrous copper sulphate, sulphur powder, talcum powder, wheat flour.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":51},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch9-041","topicId":"chem-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Uses of the forms of carbon","content":"| Form | Uses |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Diamond** | jewellery, cutting and drilling rocks and glass, bearings in watches, radiation-proof windows of satellites |\n| **Graphite** | lubricant, pencil leads, electrodes, crucibles, moderator in nuclear reactors, artificial diamonds |\n| **Fullerene** | insulators and superconductors |\n| **Coal** | fuel; coke, coal gas, coal tar; synthetic petrol, drugs, perfumes |\n| **Coke** | smokeless fuel, reducing agent, water gas and producer gas |\n| **Wood charcoal** | fuel, reducing agent, gunpowder, decolourising sugar, gas masks, tablets for stomach problems |\n| **Sugar charcoal** | reducing agent, decolourising solutions, artificial diamonds |\n| **Bone charcoal** | decolourising cane sugar, black paint, water filters |\n| **Lamp black (soot)** | shoe polish, carbon paper, paints, printing ink |\n| **Gas carbon** | electrodes of dry cells, carbon rods of arc lamps |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":52},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch9-042","topicId":"chem-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Test for carbon dioxide: it puts out a flame","content":"**Test:** lower a burning candle or smouldering matchstick into a jar of the gas.\n\n**Observation:** the flame goes out.\n\n**Conclusion:** carbon dioxide is a **non-supporter of combustion** (it also does not burn). Lime water turning milky is the confirmatory test.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":53},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch9-043","topicId":"chem-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Uses of carbon dioxide: refrigeration and storage","content":"• **Dry ice** (solid carbon dioxide) is used as a coolant and refrigerant — in ships and trucks to keep fruits, vegetables and meat from perishing.\n• **Grain storage:** grains kept in an atmosphere of carbon dioxide stay in good condition for a long time because insects cannot survive.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":54},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch9-044","topicId":"chem-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Do you know? Oxygen balance in nature","content":"• Plants **and** animals both respire all the time (24 hours).\n• Only green plants photosynthesise, and only by day.\n• Even so, the **oxygen supply stays enough for all organisms**, because photosynthesis in the day releases far more oxygen than respiration uses.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":55},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch9-045","topicId":"chem-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Carbon monoxide is poisonous","content":"• Even **0.5% carbon monoxide** in the air, if inhaled, can cause death.\n• CO combines with **haemoglobin** in red blood cells to form a stable compound, **carboxyhaemoglobin**.\n• Haemoglobin can then **no longer carry oxygen**, so body cells are starved of oxygen.\n• This paralyses the respiratory organs and causes death by **suffocation (asphyxiation)**.\n\n**Stay safe:** never run an engine in a closed garage and ensure proper ventilation when using coal or wood fires.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":56},{"id":"sup2-chem-ch9-046","topicId":"chem-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Clean cooking energy: Ujjwala Yojana","content":"Burning wood and coal indoors produces **carbon monoxide and smoke** that cause indoor air pollution.\n\nThe Government of India's **Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana** provides **LPG connections to poor households**, giving clean cooking fuel and reducing indoor air pollution.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":57},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch2-047","topicId":"bio-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Vegetative propagation: stem cutting","content":"**Stem cutting** — a piece of stem with **axillary buds** is cut and planted in moist soil.\n• A **rooting hormone** is sometimes used to help roots form.\n• New roots and shoots grow from the buds.\n• **Examples:** rose, coleus, bougainvillea, sugarcane, cactus.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":58},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch2-048","topicId":"bio-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Tissue culture (micropropagation)","content":"Growing a whole plant from a **tiny piece of tissue** in the laboratory.\n\n1. A small piece of tissue (the **explant**) is taken from a plant tip — cells there divide rapidly and are not yet specialised.\n2. It is grown in a **nutrient medium** with hormones.\n3. It forms a shapeless mass called **callus**.\n4. Small parts of the callus are moved to another medium that makes them form **plantlets**.\n5. The plantlets are **transplanted** into soil or pots.\n\nThis technique is also called **micropropagation**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":59},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch2-049","topicId":"bio-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Pollination by insects","content":"Butterflies, bees and moths visit flowers to collect **nectar**, but in doing so they carry pollen from one flower to another — they **pollinate** the flowers.\n\nInsect-pollinated flowers are usually **colourful, scented and have nectar**, which attracts insects.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":60},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch2-050","topicId":"bio-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Artificial pollination: bagging","content":"In artificial pollination (used in plant breeding):\n1. The anthers are removed from the chosen flower (**emasculation**).\n2. The emasculated flower is **covered with a polythene bag (bagging)** so no unwanted pollen reaches it.\n3. When the stigma is ready, the **desired pollen** is dusted onto it.\n4. It is bagged again until fertilisation is complete.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":61},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch3-051","topicId":"bio-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Puberty: when the body becomes sexually mature","content":"• The human reproductive system develops and works under the control of **hormones**.\n• The reproductive organs become functional only at **sexual maturity**.\n• The stage in which the body changes and becomes sexually mature is called **puberty**.\n\n| | Age of puberty |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Boys** | about 14 – 16 years |\n| **Girls** | about 11 – 13 years |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":62},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch4-052","topicId":"bio-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Tertiary consumers (top carnivores)","content":"**Tertiary consumers** (third-order consumers) feed on secondary consumers.\n\n• They include carnivores that are **not eaten by any other animal** — the **top carnivores**.\n• **Examples:** lion, leopard, tiger.\n\nFood chain example: grass → deer → tiger.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":63},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch4-053","topicId":"bio-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ How abiotic factors decide which living things survive","content":"The non-living (**abiotic**) factors — temperature, rainfall, soil — decide which living (**biotic**) things can live in an area.\n\n• **Hot and wet climate:** evergreen trees, lizards, snakes, insects and big animals like elephants, bears and tigers.\n• **Very cold climate:** polar bears, penguins, seals, Arctic hares, yaks.\n\nThis interaction keeps the **ecosystem in balance**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":64},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch4-054","topicId":"bio-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Wind as an abiotic factor","content":"**Wind** is moving air.\n\n**Effects on plants**\n• High wind increases **transpiration** — the plant loses a lot of water (desiccation).\n• Weak-rooted plants are **uprooted**; twigs break; fires spread faster.\n• Coastal trees (palm, coconut) have **deeply cut or serrated leaves** that reduce the effect of wind.\n\n**Benefits:** wind helps in **seed dispersal**, **pollination** and spreading pollutants away.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":65},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch4-055","topicId":"bio-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Temperature and where animals live","content":"Most plants and animals prefer warm places, but some thrive in the cold.\n• **Cold:** polar bears and penguins cannot survive in warm tropical jungles.\n• **Warm:** lions and elephants live in warm forests and are not found in cold regions.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":66},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch1-056","topicId":"bio-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Sunlight and plants: sleep movements","content":"Light affects the behaviour of plants.\n\n• **Portulaca (dhoop-khirri):** flowers open after sunrise and close after sunset.\n• **Bauhinia (kachnar):** leaves open in the day and fold at night.\n\nSunlight is also needed for **photosynthesis**, which is why plants grow towards light.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":67},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch1-057","topicId":"bio-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Soil as a natural resource","content":"**Soil** is the uppermost layer of the Earth's crust, formed from **weathered rocks** and decayed plants and animals (humus).\n\n**Functions of soil**\n• Gives **support and anchorage** to plants.\n• Supplies **water and minerals** to plants.\n• Provides a **home** for many organisms (earthworms, insects, microbes).\n• Recycles nutrients through decomposers.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":68},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch4-058","topicId":"bio-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Mutualism: both partners benefit","content":"**Mutualism** is a close association between two (or more) organisms of **different species** in which **all partners benefit**.\n\n**Examples:** lichen (alga + fungus), bees and flowers, nitrogen-fixing bacteria in root nodules of legumes.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":69},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch4-059","topicId":"bio-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Parasitism","content":"In **parasitism** (*para* = other) one organism (the **parasite**) lives on or in another (the **host**) and takes food from it, harming the host.\n\n| Type | Meaning | Examples |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Ectoparasite** | lives **outside** the host | ticks, lice |\n| **Endoparasite** | lives **inside** the host | tapeworm, malarial parasite |\n\n**Plant parasites:** *Cuscuta* (amarbel), sandalwood trees, *Orobanche* (broomrape).\nPlants are also attacked by bacteria, fungi, viruses, nematodes and insects.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":70},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch5-060","topicId":"bio-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Pituitary gland: gonad-stimulating hormones","content":"The **pituitary gland** secretes hormones that control the **testes and ovaries**, such as **FSH (follicle stimulating hormone)**.\n\nIt is called the **master gland** because it controls many other endocrine glands.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":71},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch5-061","topicId":"bio-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Physical changes at adolescence","content":"• **Girls:** the breasts begin to develop.\n• **Boys:** facial hair (moustache, beard) and chest hair begin to appear.\n\nThese changes are caused by **sex hormones** — oestrogen in girls and testosterone in boys.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":72},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch5-062","topicId":"bio-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Emotional changes in adolescents","content":"Adolescents are strongly influenced by **peers** and often want to try new experiences.\n\n**What helps:** healthy, safe choices and proper guidance from parents and teachers.\n\nAdolescence is a crucial period — with the right guidance, young people grow into **responsible and strong-visioned** adults.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":73},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch5-063","topicId":"bio-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Why personal hygiene matters","content":"Good personal hygiene keeps us **healthy and free from infections**.\n\nIt also builds **self-esteem** — when we take care of ourselves, we feel better about ourselves.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":74},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch5-064","topicId":"bio-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Stress management","content":"**Stress management** means the techniques that help control stress.\n\n**Ways:** meditation, yoga, exercise, good time management, listening to music, laughing, rational thinking, playing games and pursuing hobbies.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":75},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch7-065","topicId":"bio-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Cerebellum: the \"little brain\"","content":"• The **second largest** part of the brain, at the **back of the head**.\n• Divided into two **cerebellar hemispheres**; surface has furrows.\n• Called the **little brain**.\n• **Function:** controls **balance, posture and coordination** of voluntary movements.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":76},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch6-066","topicId":"bio-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Palpitation","content":"**Palpitation** is rapid, forceful beating of the heart; the person remains conscious.\n\n**Causes:** fear/danger, tension, extreme exercise (and sometimes illness).","source":"ocr_supplement","order":77},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch6-067","topicId":"bio-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Cardiac arrest","content":"**Cardiac arrest** — complete stoppage of the heart's activity; the heart stops pumping blood.\n\n• It happens when **blood supply to the heart muscle is blocked**.\n• Part of the heart muscle stops working and may be permanently damaged.\n• The blockage often arises from **cholesterol and calcium deposits** on artery walls.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":78},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch8-068","topicId":"bio-ch8","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Communicable diseases: summary table","content":"| Disease | Cause | Main symptoms | Spread | Prevention |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Influenza (flu)** | virus | fever, chills, cold, sneezing, cough, headache, weakness | droplets from coughing, sneezing, spitting; overcrowding | avoid patients, mask, vaccination, isolate the patient 10–12 days |\n| **Measles** | virus (myxovirus) | red rashes from the 3rd day, first behind the ears and face, then whole body; rashes fade leaving brown spots | coughing and sneezing; contagious 4 days before and after rash | MMR vaccination |\n| **Dengue (break-bone fever)** | dengue virus | fever, severe muscle and joint pains, rash on 4th–6th day, low platelets (<20,000 can cause bleeding) | **Aedes aegypti** mosquito (day-biter) | control mosquitoes and larvae, larvicides, awareness |\n| **Chikungunya** | alpha virus | fever, severe joint pain for months, muscle pain, headache, rash | Aedes mosquito; **not** contagious directly | no vaccine; spray insecticides, nets, *Gambusia* fish eat larvae |\n| **AIDS** | HIV | severe weight loss, damaged immunity, other infections | sexual contact, infected blood, mother to child, contaminated needles | education, sterile needles, tested blood, safe sex |\n| **Malaria** | *Plasmodium* (protozoan) | chills, shivering, high fever with headache, sweating; recurring fever | bite of female **Anopheles** mosquito | nets, repellents, chloroquine and other drugs |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":79},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch8-069","topicId":"bio-ch8","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Vectors and vector-borne diseases","content":"A **vector** carries a disease-causing organism from one host to another.\n\n| Vector | Pathogen | Disease | Symptoms |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Female **Anopheles** mosquito | *Plasmodium* (protozoan) | **Malaria** | periodic fever with chills, fall in red blood cells |\n| **Aedes** mosquito | yellow fever virus | **Yellow fever** | fever, jaundice, vomiting |\n| **Housefly** | *Entamoeba histolytica* (protozoan) | **Amoebic dysentery** | abdominal pain, severe diarrhoea with blood and mucus |\n| **Tse-tse fly** | *Trypanosoma* (protozoan) | **Sleeping sickness** | fever, rash, fatigue, damage to lymph and nervous system, sleepiness |\n| **Rat flea** | *Yersinia pestis* (bacterium) | **Plague** | swollen painful lymph nodes, sudden fever, chills, headache |\n| Saliva of infected animals (dogs) | Rhabdovirus | **Rabies** | attacks the nervous system: confusion, fear of water, paralysis, death |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":80},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch8-070","topicId":"bio-ch8","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Types of vaccines","content":"| Vaccine contains | Examples |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Killed germs** | TAB, Salk's polio vaccine, rabies vaccine |\n| **Living but weakened (attenuated) germs** | MMR, BCG |\n| **Toxoids** (harmless toxins made by the germs) | tetanus, diphtheria |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":81},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch8-071","topicId":"bio-ch8","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Animal bites and rabies","content":"• Bites can come from pet or stray dogs and cats.\n• A pet whose **immunisations are up to date** is unlikely to have rabies.\n• If it bites, **observe the animal for 10 days** — if it stays healthy, rabies injections are not needed.\n• Even so, taking anti-rabies injections is a **safe preventive step**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":82},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch9-072","topicId":"bio-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Cheese making","content":"**Cheese** is a nutritious milk product — rich in proteins, fats, calcium and vitamins.\n\n**Steps**\n1. **Curdling** of milk with the help of bacteria.\n2. Curd is processed to remove **moisture**.\n3. **Salting** — prevents growth of harmful microbes.\n4. **Ripening** — gives the cheese its typical flavour.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":83},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch9-073","topicId":"bio-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Horticultural crops","content":"| Type | Examples |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Vegetables** | potato, tomato, brinjal, cabbage, onion, spinach, lady's finger, radish, beetroot, turnip, beans |\n| **Fruits** | apple, banana, orange, grapes, guava, mango, papaya, pineapple |\n| **Flowers** | rose, jasmine, gladiolus, marigold, larkspur, balsam |\n| **Ornamental plants** | cactus, croton, bamboo, china palm, ferns |\n| **Ornamental trees** | thuja, Christmas tree, bottlebrush, royal palm, Ashoka, fishtail palm |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":84},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch9-074","topicId":"bio-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Draught animals","content":"**Draught animals** are used for **physical work** such as dragging heavy weights.\n\n• **Examples:** buffalo, bullock, horse, mule, donkey, elephant.\n• They **carry loads, pull farm implements** and move heavy devices.\n• In India bullocks, buffaloes and horses pull carts.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":85},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch9-075","topicId":"bio-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Pisciculture: why fish matter","content":"• Fish are an excellent source of **animal protein**, easy to digest.\n• **Cod and shark liver oils** are rich in **vitamin D**.\n• Fish are an important link in the **food chain** — they are eaten by frogs, snakes and birds.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":86},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch9-076","topicId":"bio-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Sericulture: silk","content":"• Silk threads are drawn from the **cocoon** spun by the silkworm.\n• Silk is **highly elastic**, absorbs water quickly and is the **longest natural fibre**.\n• **Varieties:** mulberry, muga and tasar (tussar).","source":"ocr_supplement","order":87},{"id":"sup2-bio-ch9-077","topicId":"bio-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Apiculture: honeybees and the beehive","content":"• **Honeybees** make honey and store it in their nest, the **beehive**.\n• A hive has small six-sided compartments called **combs**.\n• All **egg-laying and rearing of larvae and pupae** takes place in the combs.\n• The shape and size of the comb depend on the kind of bee.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":88},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch1-079","topicId":"geo-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Settlements: temporary and permanent","content":"**Temporary settlements** — built by people engaged in hunting and gathering, shifting cultivation and **transhumance** (seasonal movement of herds).\n• Tents and huts are common; sometimes built beside construction sites in cities.\n\n**Permanent settlements** — a temporary settlement may become permanent with time; people build permanent houses.\n• Growth depends on **availability of water and cultivable land**.\n• Roads, railways, police station, post office, hospital and market add to the growth.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":89},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch8-080","topicId":"geo-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Linear settlements","content":"**Linear settlements** form in a **line** — houses, shops, dhabas, petrol pumps and garages grow along **roads, railway lines or river banks** and gradually become larger.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":90},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch1-081","topicId":"geo-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Settlements that grow around transport","content":"• A **railway stop** grows into a settlement where a station is needed.\n• On **main roads**: dhabas, repair shops, petrol pumps develop and become travellers' stops.\n• On **rivers and coasts**: places where boats unload goods and passengers.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":91},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch1-082","topicId":"geo-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Transport and communication on a topographical map","content":"**Roads**\n• **Metalled roads** — for trucks, buses, cars.\n• **Unmetalled:** cart tracks, **pack tracks** (goods carried on mules and ponies in hills), footpaths in inaccessible hills.\n\n**Planning of roads and railways**\n• Depends on **relief and drainage**; many bridges = many streams; tunnels and cuttings = hilly terrain.\n• **Embankments** keep floodwater out and support roads/rails on soft ground; **causeways** cross small streams.\n\n**Railways in India:** three gauges — **broad, metre, narrow**. Metre gauge is being phased out; narrow gauge survives in hill stations like **Darjeeling** and **Shimla**. Double track = two sets of lines.\n\n**Airports** are marked by an aeroplane sign and found only in large cities.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":92},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch1-083","topicId":"geo-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Conventional signs and grid references","content":"**Conventional signs on a topo sheet (a few):** state and district boundary, metalled road, unmetalled road, pack track, cart track, foot track, river, post office, police station, dispensary, hospital, temple, church, mosque, wooded land, contour lines, huts, tank, reserved forest (RF), protected forest (PF).\n\n• **Reserved forest:** for scientific study and conservation; entry barred.\n• **Protected forest:** under forest authorities; locals may do forestry but **logging is prohibited**.\n\n**Grid references**\n• Four-figure and six-figure references have 4 and 6 digits.\n• Each grid square is **1 km × 1 km** on the ground (2 cm × 2 cm on the map). Eastings 38–42 and northings 94–98 enclose 4 × 4 = **16 sq. km**.\n\n**Legend (key):** always read it first to interpret relief, drainage, land use, transport and settlements.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":93},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch2-084","topicId":"geo-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ What is population?","content":"**Population** is the number of inhabitants of a country or region.\n• The world has **over 8 billion** people and is growing.\n• Total land area is **510 million sq. km**; humans occupy about **64 million sq. km** (the continental masses).\n• Population growth has reduced the numbers of wild animals like elephants, tigers and polar bears.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":94},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch2-085","topicId":"geo-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Population by continent","content":"| Continent | Population (approx.) |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Asia** | over 4.7 billion (largest) |\n| **Africa** | 1.46 billion |\n| **Europe** | 741 million |\n| **North America** | 604 million |\n| **South America** | 439 million |\n| **Oceania** | about 44 million (Australia, New Zealand and Pacific islands) |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":95},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch2-086","topicId":"geo-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Ageing population in developed countries","content":"In developed countries **death rates have fallen and life expectancy has risen** thanks to better medical facilities. So the number of **elderly people has grown**, which puts a **financial burden on governments** (pensions and care).","source":"ocr_supplement","order":96},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch4-087","topicId":"geo-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Impact of overpopulation: cities and the planet","content":"**On people:** shortage of houses and jobs → homeless and unemployed people, **slums**, hunger, malnutrition, disease, illiteracy.\n\n**On the planet**\n• Humans have already used about **35% of the planet's resources**.\n• Farmland spreads at the cost of **forests, meadows and rivers**, destroying habitats.\n• More industries cause **air, water and soil pollution** and loss of biodiversity.\n• A reminder: **there is no Planet B**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":97},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch2-088","topicId":"geo-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Knowledge of India: Registrar General and Census Commissioner","content":"The **Registrar General and Census Commissioner of India** (Ministry of Home Affairs):\n• conducts the **decennial census** — population, demographics, socio-economic data;\n• maintains **vital statistics** (births and deaths), housing and language data;\n• supports **research, planning and resource allocation**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":98},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch2-089","topicId":"geo-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Causes of underpopulation","content":"**Underpopulation** can be caused by:\n• **Natural disasters** (earthquakes, tsunamis, floods) and **man-made disasters** such as war.\n• **Epidemics** — cholera, malaria, typhoid, Covid-19.\n• **Falling birth rates** — Italy, Spain, Romania, Scandinavian countries.\n• **Genetic reasons / infertility** — e.g. South Korea, Japan.\n• High **emigration**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":99},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch8-090","topicId":"geo-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Age and sex composition: sex ratio","content":"• **Sex ratio** = number of females per 1,000 males.\n• World average: **984** females per 1,000 males.\n• Lower than average: **India, Pakistan, China, Saudi Arabia, Afghanistan**.\n• Lowest in the world: **Qatar** (334); highest: **Latvia** (1,173).","source":"ocr_supplement","order":100},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch2-091","topicId":"geo-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Thomas Malthus and his population theory","content":"**Thomas Robert Malthus (1766–1834)** — English economist and demographer.\n• **Theory:** population tends to grow faster than the food supply, until **famine, disease, war or calamity** reverse the growth.\n• He also proposed an exponential formula to forecast population — the **Malthusian growth model**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":101},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch3-092","topicId":"geo-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Migration: a normal human behaviour","content":"• Migration is a **global phenomenon**.\n• Prehistoric humans were **hunter-gatherers**, always moving for food and shelter; floods, storms and earthquakes also forced them to move.\n• With the beginning of **agriculture**, people started to settle in one place.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":102},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch3-093","topicId":"geo-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Emigration","content":"**Emigration:** leaving one's own country to settle in another. The person is an **emigrant** from the home country.\n\n*Example:* a person who leaves India for Canada is said to have emigrated from India (and is an immigrant in Canada).","source":"ocr_supplement","order":103},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch3-094","topicId":"geo-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Rural-to-rural migration","content":"Movement from one rural area to another — for **property, marriage, better resources**, etc.\n\n*Example:* in rural India, girls usually move to their husband's village after **marriage**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":104},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch3-095","topicId":"geo-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Push factor: war","content":"War pushes people out: in the violent war in **Syria**, thousands of Syrians made difficult journeys to safer neighbouring and distant countries.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":105},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch2-096","topicId":"geo-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Push factor: famine and political instability","content":"Food and essentials become scarce and costly during famine or political instability:\n• **Venezuela, 2016** — people crossed into Colombia for food and medicine.\n• **Greece, 2015** — economic crisis caused shortages of food and medicine.\n• **Nigeria, 2016** — severe grain shortage sent people to neighbouring countries.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":106},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch3-097","topicId":"geo-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Push factor: religion","content":"People move to **escape religious persecution** and live where they can practise their faith safely.\n\n*Examples:* movement of **Jews to Israel**; the huge migration between **India and Pakistan at Partition**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":107},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch3-098","topicId":"geo-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Push factor: politics","content":"People may leave a country because they dislike its **form of government**, to avoid a **new regime** or a **war**, or because they lost homes and farms in war.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":108},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch3-099","topicId":"geo-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Impact of migration","content":"Migration affects **both the destination country and the home country**, in positive and negative ways.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":109},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch3-100","topicId":"geo-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Positive impacts of migration","content":"• **Cheap labour:** migrants take unskilled jobs at lower wages (e.g. Indians in the UAE and Saudi Arabia).\n• **Return of skills:** returning migrants bring **knowledge, skills and ideas** and start businesses — in **Kerala**, returnees from the Gulf started restaurants, schools and shopping malls, creating jobs.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":110},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch3-101","topicId":"geo-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Negative impact: job loss for locals","content":"Migrants willing to work for **lower pay** can take jobs from locals — in the **USA**, many resent migrants from South and Southeast Asia and Latin America for this reason.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":111},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch3-102","topicId":"geo-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Quick facts: Indian diaspora and demand","content":"• India has the **largest transnational population**: about **35 lakh in the UAE, 27 lakh in the USA, 25 lakh in Saudi Arabia**.\n• Business grows with bigger markets. When young people **leave**, the population stalls and **demand for goods and services falls**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":112},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch8-103","topicId":"geo-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Social cost of migration: family life","content":"Young parents often leave **children and elderly dependents behind**. The children can suffer **deep psychological scars**, miss love and care and be exposed to social vices; the elderly feel left out and neglected.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":113},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch3-104","topicId":"geo-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Brain drain: push and pull factors","content":"**Push factors (home country)**\n• No suitably paying jobs, poverty, no prospect of a better standard of living.\n• Political instability, favouritism, religious and social discrimination.\n\n**Pull factors (destination)**\n• Higher income and comfortable lifestyle.\n• Better educational opportunities, training and research.\n• Promotion without partiality; a stable, liberal government.\n• Better working conditions and medical facilities.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":114},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch3-105","topicId":"geo-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Knowledge of India: tackling brain drain","content":"The Government of India has:\n• promoted **higher education and research** — new **IITs and AIIMS**;\n• offered **scholarships and incentives** so that talented students and researchers stay and contribute to India's growth.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":115},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch3-106","topicId":"geo-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Biography: Ravenstein and John Graunt","content":"**Ernst Georg Ravenstein** (Anglo-German geographer) — in **1876, 1885 and 1889** published papers on migration and outlined **\"laws of migration\"** to explain and predict migration patterns.\n\n**John Graunt (1620–74)** — English statistician, founder of **demography and vital statistics**; made the **first mortality table** (*London Bills of Mortality*) and first documented **excess deaths** in epidemics.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":116},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch3-107","topicId":"geo-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Important term: persecute","content":"**Persecute:** to treat someone cruelly and unfairly because of their race, religion or political beliefs.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":117},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch3-109","topicId":"geo-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Causes of urbanization: attractions of the city","content":"• Cities offer **entertainment** — cinemas, plays, sports, fairs, exhibitions.\n• **Modern conveniences**, better lifestyles and **freedom from rigid social norms** such as the caste system (strong in villages) draw people to cities.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":118},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch8-110","topicId":"geo-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Positive impact of urbanization: new skills","content":"Urbanization helps people acquire **modern communication skills** — computers, smartphones and new languages. Thousands of young people found jobs in call centres across Indian cities because of **spoken and written English**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":119},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch4-111","topicId":"geo-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Sustainable and environment-friendly cities","content":"Governments should plan **sustainable cities**: safe, clean and healthy.\n• Promote **smart growth** and pass supporting laws.\n• Invest in **green infrastructure**, eco-friendly industries, recycling and environmental campaigns.\n• Manage pollution; use **renewable energy**; eco-friendly public transport; **water recycling** and land reclamation.\n• Encourage **electric vehicles, electric buses, trams and metro trains**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":120},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch4-112","topicId":"geo-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Creating more jobs to reduce urban problems","content":"• Create **more employment** to ease the negative effects of rapid urbanization.\n• Encourage **private investment** and optimal use of natural resources; promote **tourism**.\n• Give **subsidies and grants** to foreign and private investment in eco-friendly projects.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":121},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch4-113","topicId":"geo-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Knowledge of India: HRIDAY scheme","content":"**HRIDAY** (Heritage City Development and Augmentation Yojana), launched in **2015** by the Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs:\n• conserves **heritage** and promotes inclusive, sustainable development of heritage sites — citizens, tourists and local business, not just monuments;\n• **Cities:** Ajmer, Amritsar, Amaravati, Warangal, Puri, Kanchipuram, Varanasi, Velankanni.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":122},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch4-114","topicId":"geo-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Satellite cities","content":"**Satellite towns/cities** are socially and economically **independent** (completely or partly) of the main city.\n• They have their own shopping centres, markets, clubs, cinemas, museums, hospitals, nursing homes and fire brigades.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":123},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch4-115","topicId":"geo-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ AMRUT and the Smart Cities Mission","content":"**AMRUT** (Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformation): improves **water supply, sewerage and storm-water drainage**, green spaces and public transport.\n\n**Smart Cities Mission:** **100 cities** selected (as of 2023), including Bhubaneswar, Jaipur, Surat, Pune, Lucknow, Chandigarh, Hyderabad, Vadodara, Kochi, Visakhapatnam, Guwahati, Raipur, Indore, Gwalior, Jabalpur and Bhopal.\n\n**Smart City Index 2023 (IMD, Zurich):** 1st **Zurich**, 2nd **Oslo**, 3rd **Canberra**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":124},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch4-116","topicId":"geo-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ How the Smart City Index is made","content":"The index uses a survey of **over 20,000 citizens** about affordable housing, traffic congestion, green spaces, jobs, technology built to address their concerns, and comfort with technologies such as face recognition and sharing personal data to reduce congestion.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":125},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch8-117","topicId":"geo-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Biography: Ildefons Cerdà, who coined \"urbanization\"","content":"**Ildefons Cerdà (1815–76)**, a Spanish engineer, wrote *General Theory of Urbanization* (**1867**) — the paper in which the word **\"urbanization\"** was invented. Today it describes the **expansion and redevelopment of cities**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":126},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-118","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Types of disasters","content":"| Natural disasters | Man-made disasters |\n| --- | --- |\n| Earthquakes, volcanic activity, tsunamis, landslides, avalanches, floods, droughts, wildfires, cyclones/storms, hurricanes | Wars, terrorism, cyber attacks, transport accidents, industrial accidents, pollution, water contamination |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":127},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-119","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Floods: effects","content":"A **flood** is a sudden influx of water that submerges land, endangering life and property.\n• Forces people to **move**; washes away the fertile **top soil**.\n• Belongings are damaged and **crops ruined**.\n• People who cannot reach safety in time **lose their lives**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":128},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-120","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Droughts: causes and effects","content":"**Cause:** insufficient rainfall for a long period.\n\n**Effects:** fall in the **water table**, **crop failure**, shortage of **food, fodder and jobs**; people starve and **migrate**, becoming homeless.\n• Worst hit: villagers dependent on **agriculture and animal husbandry**, and **tribals** dependent on forests.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":129},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-121","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Fires","content":"• The **most common** kind of disaster; can break out at homes, offices, hospitals, recreation centres.\n• More casualties in **crowded places with few exits** — e.g. the **Uphaar cinema tragedy, Delhi, 13 July 1997**.\n• **Causes:** poor wiring and faulty electrical equipment (the commonest cause), cooking-gas leakage, a careless matchstick.\n• **Prevention:** inspect wiring regularly and replace worn-out wires with standard-quality fittings.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":130},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-122","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Nuclear disasters","content":"• Caused by **nuclear weapons**, weapons of mass destruction (WMD).\n• A nuclear explosion is blindingly bright, followed by a **heat wave** and a huge cloud.\n• **Radioactivity** (energy given out by uranium, plutonium, etc.) is the **silent killer** — nausea, dizziness, vomiting, disorientation, **cancer**, and effects that last for a very long time.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":131},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-123","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Chemical and biological disasters","content":"**Biological disasters** are caused by **germs developed in laboratories** and released into the air.\n• They infect through **inhalation or a cut or wound**.\n• A few drops of the **smallpox or plague virus** could start an epidemic and kill thousands.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":132},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-124","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Oil spills","content":"Oil spills happen when large quantities of oil are released into seas during **drilling, tanker cleaning, pipeline or storage-tank leaks**, or in an **accident or explosion on a tanker or rig**.\n\n**Effects:** chemicals kill marine life and **coral reefs**; black residue coats **bird feathers and animal fur**; fishing and tourism are destroyed when oil reaches the coast. The 2010 **Deepwater Horizon** is among the worst.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":133},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-125","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Knowledge of India: \"the man of the golden hour\"","content":"**Suraj Prakash Vaid**, a 66-year-old Delhi taxi driver, is called **\"the man of the golden hour\"**.\n• He carries a **first-aid kit** and rides a scooter to beat traffic.\n• He helps accident victims in the crucial **first hour** and has saved many lives.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":134},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-126","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Terror attacks","content":"Disasters caused by **explosives, guns, or chemical and biological weapons**, by people who wrongly believe social, political or religious goals can be achieved by killing.\n• Terrorists also **hijack planes** and destroy infrastructure such as **railway tracks and bridges**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":135},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-127","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Travel-related disasters","content":"**Examples:** air crashes, train collisions and derailments, boats and ships capsizing.\n\n**Causes:** equipment failure, loss of radar contact, brake failure, **human error** (driver asleep, missed signal), fuel leakage, or natural events (earthquakes, storms, landslides).","source":"ocr_supplement","order":136},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-128","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ The Nepal earthquake (2015): facts","content":"• Magnitude **7.8**.\n• Epicentre about **77 km north-west of Kathmandu**; focus about **15 km underground**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":137},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-129","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Floods in Bihar","content":"**Why Bihar floods so often:** rivers like the **Kosi, Bagmati and Gandak** come down from high, mountainous **Nepal** and reach the plains of Bihar over a short distance, making them very forceful.\n\n**Latest flood:** 60+ deaths, **2.9 crore people** affected, districts like Purnea, Kishanganj, Araria, Madhepura, Darbhanga, Madhubani, Supaul, Bhagalpur and Muzaffarpur hit; **130+ villages** inundated, **52,000+ displaced**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":138},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-130","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Biography: John Milne","content":"**John Milne (1850–1913)** — British geologist and seismologist.\n• Built the first **international network** for seismological data.\n• Made the world's **first modern seismograph (1880)** — a horizontal pendulum that recorded vibrations along fault lines.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":139},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-133","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Hiroshima and Nagasaki (1945): long-term effects","content":"The atomic bombings of **Hiroshima and Nagasaki (August 1945)** caused immediate devastation and **severe, long-lasting biological effects** — intense radiation harmed people and the environment, affecting even the survivors' **descendants**. They highlight the need to protect life and the environment.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":140},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-135","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Safety measures during earthquakes","content":"**Indoors:** **Drop, Cover, Hold** — drop to the ground, take cover under a bed or table, hold on.\n\n**Outdoors:** move to an **open area** away from trees, buildings, transmission towers and electricity poles. Stay away from **bridges and flyovers**, even after the shaking stops.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":141},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-136","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Preventing drought: rainwater harvesting","content":"**Rainwater harvesting** replenishes the falling groundwater. Governments and social organisations should **teach and enable people** to harvest rainwater and restore water reserves.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":142},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-137","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Safety measures during cyclones","content":"• Track **warnings** from the meteorological department, the **Disaster Warning System**, TV and radio, and follow instructions.\n• A timely warning saves many lives.\n• *Example:* **Cyclone Biparjoy (2023)** hit Gujarat; timely preparation by agencies and forces prevented large-scale damage.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":143},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-138","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Preventing fires: everyday precautions","content":"• Dispose of matchsticks **properly** after lighting a candle or stove.\n• If a fire breaks out nearby, **call the fire brigade and the police immediately**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":144},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-139","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Role of the government in disaster safety","content":"• Send **inspection teams** to hotels, theatres, hospitals and check safety rules.\n• Check **building plans** (especially multistorey) for defects and ban **substandard material**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":145},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-140","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Role of social organisations","content":"The **Red Cross**, UN agencies and **NGOs** provide emergency **shelter, food and medicines** and reduce victims' feelings of **isolation and abandonment**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":146},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-141","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Disaster management at a glance","content":"| Safety measures | Prevention | Role of government and social agencies |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Keep an **emergency kit** (food, water, first-aid, torch, blanket) | **Identify hazards** and reduce them; retrofit buildings, relocate people | Make **disaster management plans** at district, state, national and village levels |\n| Know **emergency exits** and a plan of where to go | **Educate the public** through training and awareness | **Coordinate relief** with agencies, NGOs, international bodies |\n| Create **safe shelter areas** and gathering points | Hold **mock drills** (fire, earthquake evacuation) | **Financial assistance**, food distribution, psychological support |\n| Stay informed through news and **alerts** | Enforce **building codes**, land-use rules and conservation | Set up and maintain emergency services |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":147},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch5-142","topicId":"geo-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Values: George Manby and the fire extinguisher","content":"**George William Manby (1765–1854)**, English inventor, made the first modern **fire extinguisher (the \"Extincteur\", 1813)** — a copper vessel with 3 gallons of potassium-carbonate solution under compressed air. It greatly improved firefighting and saved lives.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":148},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch6-143","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Asia at a glance (fact file)","content":"| Feature | Fact |\n| --- | --- |\n| Latitudinal extent | 10° S – 80° N |\n| Longitudinal extent | 25° E – 170° W |\n| Widest distance | 8,690 km (N–S), 9,700 km (E–W) |\n| Coastline | 129,077 km |\n| Number of countries | 48 |\n| Largest country | Russia |\n| Smallest country | Maldives |\n| Most industrialised | Japan |\n| Highest peak | Mt Everest (8,848.86 m) |\n| Highest plateau | Plateau of Tibet — \"roof of the world\" |\n| Lowest point | Dead Sea (400 m below sea level) |\n| Deepest lake | Lake Baikal |\n| Longest strait | Strait of Malacca |\n| Coldest place | Verkhoyansk, Russia (−70 °C) |\n| Warmest place | Dasht-e-Lut, Iran (70.7 °C surface, 2021) |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":149},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch6-144","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Central Asia","content":"• **Landlocked** countries: Kazakhstan, Uzbekistan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan, Turkmenistan and Afghanistan.\n• Extends from the **Caspian Sea** to the border of western China.\n• **More than 60% desert, 40% grassland**; has the **Altai Mountains**.\n• Two main rivers: **Amu Darya** and **Syr Darya** — vital because the region is dry.\n• Population about **67.3 million**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":150},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch6-145","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ South-East Asia","content":"• Island countries: **Malaysia, Singapore, Brunei, Indonesia, Timor-Leste, Philippines** (plus mainland countries).\n• Bounded by the **Indian Ocean** (west) and **Pacific Ocean** (east).\n• Rich in **tropical rainforests** and wildlife.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":151},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch6-146","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ West Asia","content":"• Countries: Armenia, UAE, Yemen, Azerbaijan, Bahrain, Lebanon, Oman, Cyprus, Syria, Türkiye, Georgia, Iran, Israel, Jordan, Kuwait, Qatar, Saudi Arabia (and Iraq).\n• Population about **257 million**; **thinly populated** because of the harsh, inhospitable climate.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":152},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch6-147","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Quick facts: Dead Sea and capitals of Asia","content":"**Dead Sea:** its salt concentration is so high that you **float** (natural buoyancy); fish and plants cannot live there — hence \"dead\".\n\n| Country | Capital | Country | Capital |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Afghanistan | Kabul | Lebanon | Beirut |\n| Armenia | Yerevan | Malaysia | Kuala Lumpur |\n| Azerbaijan | Baku | Maldives | Malé |\n| Bahrain | Manama | Mongolia | Ulaanbaatar |\n| Bangladesh | Dhaka | Myanmar | Naypyidaw |\n| Bhutan | Thimphu | Nepal | Kathmandu |\n| Brunei | Bandar Seri Begawan | Oman | Muscat |\n| Cambodia | Phnom Penh | Pakistan | Islamabad |\n| China | Beijing | Philippines | Manila |\n| Cyprus | Nicosia | Qatar | Doha |\n| Georgia | Tbilisi | Russia | Moscow |\n| India | New Delhi | Saudi Arabia | Riyadh |\n| Indonesia | Jakarta | Singapore | Singapore |\n| Iran | Tehran | Sri Lanka | Sri Jayawardenepura Kotte |\n| Iraq | Baghdad | Syria | Damascus |\n| Israel | Jerusalem | Tajikistan | Dushanbe |\n| Japan | Tokyo | Thailand | Bangkok |\n| Jordan | Amman | Timor-Leste | Dili |\n| Kazakhstan | Astana | Türkiye | Ankara |\n| North Korea | Pyongyang | Turkmenistan | Ashgabat |\n| South Korea | Seoul | UAE | Abu Dhabi |\n| Kuwait | Kuwait City | Uzbekistan | Tashkent |\n| Kyrgyzstan | Bishkek | Vietnam | Hanoi |\n| Laos | Vientiane | Yemen | Sana'a |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":153},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch6-148","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ The Northern Lowlands (Great Siberian Plain)","content":"• Stretch from the **Ural mountains** in the west to the **Bering Strait** in the north-east, between the **Arctic Ocean** and the Central Mountains.\n• Triangular, widest in the west; the **world's greatest continuous plains**.\n• Built by the **Ob, Yenisey and Lena** rivers flowing north into the Arctic Ocean.\n• **Marshy and swampy**; in winter the lower courses freeze, so water from warmer upper courses spreads over large areas.\n• A smaller lowland lies around the **Aral Sea** in Central Asia.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":154},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch6-149","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Mountain ranges radiating from the Pamir Knot","content":"The **Pamir Knot** is the hub from which Asia's great ranges spread.\n\n| Direction | Ranges |\n| --- | --- |\n| **East / north** | Kunlun, Tien Shan; then **Altai, Yablonovy, Stanovoy** to the north-east corner; **Khingan** (N–S, near the coast) |\n| **South-east** | **Himalayas** (arc across northern India, turning south into Myanmar as the **Arakan Yoma**); **Karakoram** between the Kunlun and the Himalayas |\n| **West** | **Hindu Kush** → **Elburz**; **Sulaiman** → **Kirthar** and **Zagros** — these enclose the **Plateau of Iran** and meet at the **Armenian Knot** |\n\nThe **Plateau of Tibet** lies between the Kunlun and the Himalayas.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":155},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch6-150","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Did you know? Passes, peaks and glaciers","content":"• **Passes** such as the **Khyber, Bolan and Sulaiman** allow movement of goods and people across the mountains.\n• **Mt Everest** (8,848.86 m, Himalayas) is the highest peak; **K2 / Godwin Austen** (8,611 m, Karakoram) is the second highest. Many peaks exceed 8,000 m.\n• Huge glaciers: **Siachen** and **Baltoro**.\n\n**India's extremes:** hottest — **Phalodi** (Rajasthan, 51 °C); coldest — **Dras** (Ladakh, about −45 °C); wettest — **Mawsynram** (Meghalaya, about 1,187 cm).","source":"ocr_supplement","order":156},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch6-151","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Important rivers of Asia","content":"| River | Direction of flow | Flows into |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Huang He | east | Gulf of Pohai (Bo Hai) |\n| Yangtze (Chang Jiang) | north-east / east | East China Sea |\n| Si Kiang (Xi) | east | South China Sea |\n| Salween | south | Gulf of Martaban |\n| Irrawaddy | south | Andaman Sea |\n| Brahmaputra | west, then south | Bay of Bengal |\n| Ganga | south-east | Bay of Bengal |\n| Indus | south-west | Arabian Sea |\n| Tigris and Euphrates | south | Persian Gulf |\n| Syr Darya and Amu Darya | north-west | Aral Sea |\n| Ob, Yenisey, Lena | north | Arctic Ocean |\n\n**Plains:** large plains are formed by the Yangtze, Huang He and Si Kiang; small plains by the Irrawaddy, Salween, Mekong and Amur.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":157},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch6-152","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Knowledge of India: culture and the Thar Desert","content":"• India is the **birthplace of Hinduism, Buddhism and Sikhism**; famous for the Konark Sun Temple, the Taj Mahal, the Himalayas and Kerala's backwaters.\n• In 2023 it landed **Chandrayaan-3** and hosted the **G20 Summit**.\n• **The Thar Desert was once green** — ancient riverbeds, lakes and fossils show it had extensive grasslands, flora and fauna.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":158},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-153","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Island arcs of Asia","content":"Groups of islands form **arcs (archipelagoes)** along Asia's Pacific edge: the **Kuril Islands**, **Japanese islands** (Hokkaido, Honshu, Shikoku, Kyushu), **Ryukyu**, the **Philippines** and **Indonesia**.\n\nIn South Asia: the **Andaman and Nicobar Islands, Sri Lanka, the Maldives and Lakshadweep**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":159},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-154","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Biography: Vasco da Gama and Dev Raj Sikka","content":"**Vasco da Gama (1460–1524)** — Portuguese navigator, first to sail from Europe to India around the **Cape of Good Hope**; reached **Calicut on 20 May 1498**, opening a **direct sea route** for spice and textile trade.\n\n**Dev Raj Sikka (1934–2017)** — meteorologist called the **\"Monsoon Man\"**; first to link **El Niño** with the Indian monsoon.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":160},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch6-155","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Asia: summer conditions","content":"• From April to June the sun's rays fall **directly on the northern hemisphere**; in June it is overhead at the **Tropic of Cancer**.\n• Most of Asia (except the far north) is warm — average about **32 °C**.\n• The world's highest recorded surface temperature (**71 °C**) was at **Dasht-e-Lut, Iran**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":161},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch6-156","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Asia: winter conditions","content":"• From October to March the northern hemisphere gets **slanting rays** because of the tilt of the Earth's axis.\n• Temperatures fall to 0 °C and below in northern Asia.\n• **Verkhoyansk (Russia)** averages about **−51 °C in January** — one of the coldest inhabited places in the world.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":162},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-157","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Tropical deciduous (monsoon) forests of India","content":"• Hardwood, broad-leaved trees that **shed leaves in the dry season** (winter).\n• **Trees:** teak, shisham, mango; **grasses and thorny bushes** in places.\n• **Wildlife:** elephants, rhinoceros, bears, deer, apes, lions, tigers, leopards.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":163},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch6-158","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Natural vegetation and wildlife of Asia by region","content":"| Region | Vegetation | Wildlife |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Hot deserts (e.g. Arabia)** | thorny bushes, shrubs, cacti, dry grass; **date palms** at oases | lizards, insects, rodents; camels |\n| **Warm temperate, eastern margin** | mixed evergreen and deciduous forests — oak, camellia, magnolia, chestnut, camphor, beech, fir; bamboo and mulberry | monkeys, foxes; parrots, peacocks, pheasants (on highland slopes) |\n| **Tibet (cold desert)** | scattered tufts of coarse grass, stunted thorny bushes | lizards, insects, rodents; the **yak** is the main domestic animal |\n| **Mediterranean** | long roots and spongy bark; laurel, acacia, oak, chestnut, walnut, olive; orchards and vineyards | little wildlife — mostly donkeys, cattle, sheep |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":164},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch6-159","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Mediterranean climate","content":"**Warm, dry summers and cool, wet winters.** Named after the **Mediterranean region**.\n\nFound in West Asian countries such as **Syria, Türkiye, Lebanon and Israel**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":165},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch6-160","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Temperate grasslands: vegetation and wildlife","content":"• Vast stretches of grassland have been converted into rich **farmland** growing **wheat, barley** and other grains.\n• **Livestock rearing** (cattle, sheep, horses) is important.\n• The two-humped **Bactrian camel** is the well-known animal of this region.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":166},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch6-161","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Goal 15: Life on land — the snow leopard","content":"• Snow leopards live in the **high mountains of northern and central Asia**; thick fur and very long tails.\n• They are **poached** for fur — 200 to 400 are killed every year for illegal trade.\n• **IUCN** lists them as **vulnerable** and works to conserve them and develop nearby communities.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":167},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch6-162","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Forests of Asia and their products","content":"About **one-third of Asia** is forested.\n\n• **Equatorial rainforests and monsoon forests:** hardwood, bamboo, sandalwood, camphor; **lac, resin, turpentine, honey, medicinal plants**; **rubber** (latex of rubber trees).\n• **Taiga / coniferous forests (northern Asia, Siberia):** softwood (birch, pine, cedar, fir) for **paper, pulp, newsprint, matchsticks, rayon**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":168},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch6-163","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Minerals and power resources of Asia","content":"**Minerals**\n• **Manganese** (India, Kazakhstan, China, Russia) — hardens steel.\n• **Bauxite** (India, Indonesia, Russia) — ore of aluminium.\n• **Mica** (India) — electrical insulator.\n• **Tin** (Malaysia, China, Myanmar, Indonesia, Thailand).\n• Gold, silver and copper in significant amounts.\n\n**Power**\n• **Petroleum:** Saudi Arabia, UAE, Bahrain, Kuwait, Iran, Iraq, India, Malaysia, Indonesia, Myanmar, China, Russia.\n• **Thermal power** from coal and petroleum.\n• **Hydroelectricity** — Russia, Japan, China, Pakistan, India; huge unused potential.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":169},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch6-164","topicId":"geo-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Biography: Middendorff","content":"**Alexander von Middendorff** — zoologist and explorer of **Siberia and the Russian Arctic**; his **1843–45 expedition** to the far north-east of Siberia helped explain how **permafrost (frozen ground)** affects the spread of plants and animals.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":170},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-166","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ India as a subcontinent","content":"• India is part of **Asia**, the **seventh-largest country** by area and **most populous** in the world.\n• The **Himalayas** form a protective barrier that isolated it from the rest of Asia and gave it a **distinct physical and cultural identity**.\n• India, Pakistan, Nepal, Bhutan, Bangladesh and Sri Lanka form the **Indian subcontinent**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":171},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-167","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ India: location and its advantages","content":"• South of the **Tropic of Cancer** India is a **peninsula** — a triangular landmass projecting into the **Indian Ocean**, with the **Arabian Sea** (west), **Bay of Bengal** (east) and **Indian Ocean** (south).\n• Seas on three sides encouraged **international trade**; India has had links with many countries since ancient times.\n• Its **central position between east and west** helped trade and industry.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":172},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-168","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ States and Union Territories of India (with capitals)","content":"| State | Capital | State | Capital |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Andhra Pradesh | Amaravati | Maharashtra | Mumbai |\n| Arunachal Pradesh | Itanagar | Manipur | Imphal |\n| Assam | Dispur | Meghalaya | Shillong |\n| Bihar | Patna | Mizoram | Aizawl |\n| Chhattisgarh | Raipur | Nagaland | Kohima |\n| Goa | Panaji | Odisha | Bhubaneswar |\n| Gujarat | Gandhinagar | Punjab | Chandigarh |\n| Haryana | Chandigarh | Rajasthan | Jaipur |\n| Himachal Pradesh | Shimla | Sikkim | Gangtok |\n| Jharkhand | Ranchi | Tamil Nadu | Chennai |\n| Karnataka | Bengaluru | Telangana | Hyderabad |\n| Kerala | Thiruvananthapuram | Tripura | Agartala |\n| Madhya Pradesh | Bhopal | Uttar Pradesh | Lucknow |\n| Uttarakhand | Dehradun | West Bengal | Kolkata |\n\n| Union Territory | Capital |\n| --- | --- |\n| Andaman & Nicobar Islands | Sri Vijaya Puram (Port Blair) |\n| Chandigarh | Chandigarh |\n| Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & Diu | Daman |\n| Delhi | New Delhi |\n| Jammu & Kashmir | Srinagar (summer), Jammu (winter) |\n| Ladakh | Leh |\n| Lakshadweep | Kavaratti |\n| Puducherry | Puducherry |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":173},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-169","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ The Northern Mountains: the Himalayas","content":"• *Himalaya* = **\"abode of snow\"**; the **highest mountain system in the world**.\n• Extend from **Nanga Parbat** (north-west) to **Namcha Barwa** (Arunachal Pradesh) — over **2,400 km**; the arc is **15–400 km wide**.\n• **Passes:** Shipki La, Thaga La, Nathu La, Jelep La.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":174},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-170","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ The three Himalayan ranges","content":"| | **Greater Himalaya (Himadri)** | **Lesser Himalaya (Himachal)** | **Outer Himalaya (Shiwaliks)** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Position | northernmost | between Greater and Outer | southern foothills |\n| Height | over 8,000 m peaks; **Everest 8,848.86 m** | up to about 5,000 m | up to about 1,500 m |\n| Width | — | about 75 km | 15–50 km |\n| Features | snow-covered all year; source of Ganga (Gangotri) and Yamuna (Yamunotri) | **Kashmir, Kullu, Kangra valleys**; hill stations Darjeeling, Shimla, Nainital, Mussoorie | flat valleys called **Duns**; rich in timber |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":175},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-171","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ The Great Northern Plains: the Ganga plains","content":"• Formed by **alluvium** deposited by the **Ganga and its tributaries** — Yamuna, Ghaghara, Gomti, Gandak, Chambal, Betwa, Son.\n• Lie in **Uttar Pradesh, Bihar and West Bengal**; average height **30–150 m**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":176},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-172","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ The coastal plains","content":"| | **Western coastal plains** | **Eastern coastal plains** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Along | **Arabian Sea** | **Bay of Bengal** |\n| Width | narrower, uneven | wider |\n| Names | **Konkan** (Maharashtra), **Kanara** (Karnataka), **Malabar** (Kerala) | **Northern Circars** and **Coromandel Coast** |\n| States | Gujarat, Maharashtra, Goa, Karnataka, Kerala | Odisha, Andhra Pradesh, Tamil Nadu |\n| Ports | Mormugao, Mangalore, Kochi | Visakhapatnam, Paradip, Chennai, Tuticorin |\n\n(Gujarat's coast also has the Luni, a small seasonal river, and the salt lake **Sambhar** in the nearby desert region.)","source":"ocr_supplement","order":177},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-173","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Quick facts: how the Himalayas formed","content":"• The Himalayas are **young fold mountains**, still **rising**.\n• About **185 million years ago** the **Tethys Sea** lay between **Laurasia** (north) and **Gondwanaland** (south).\n• The two landmasses moved closer and **collided**, folding the sediments of the sea bed upwards to form the mountains.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":178},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-174","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ The Himalayan rivers: Ganga and Brahmaputra systems","content":"**Ganga:** rises from the **Gangotri Glacier**.\n• Left-bank tributaries: Ramganga, Gomti, Ghaghara, Gandak, Kosi (from the Himalayas).\n• Right-bank tributaries: Chambal, Betwa, Son (from the peninsula); only the **Yamuna** (from Yamunotri) is a Himalayan right-bank tributary.\n\n**Brahmaputra** (2,700 km): rises near **Mansarovar** and flows east through Tibet as the **Tsang Po**, enters India in Arunachal Pradesh.\n• Right-bank tributaries: Tista, Barali, Torsa, Subansiri; left-bank: Buri-Dihing, Disang, Kopili, Dhansiri.\n• Upper course in **Assam**; in Bangladesh it joins the Ganga; both build a fertile **delta** and fall into the **Bay of Bengal**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":179},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-175","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ West-flowing rivers: Narmada and Tapi","content":"• **Narmada:** rises near the **Amarkantak plateau**, flows between the **Vindhya and Satpura** ranges and falls into the **Gulf of Khambhat** near Bharuch (Gujarat).\n• **Tapi (724 km):** rises in hills of Madhya Pradesh, flows south of the Satpuras and drains into the **Arabian Sea** near **Surat**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":180},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-176","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ East-flowing rivers: the Godavari","content":"**Godavari (1,465 km)** — the **longest river of the peninsular plateau**, called the **\"Ganga of the South\"**.\n• Rises near **Nashik** (Maharashtra) and flows through Maharashtra and Andhra Pradesh into the **Bay of Bengal**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":181},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-177","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Other rivers of the peninsula","content":"• **East-flowing** (into the Bay of Bengal): **Pennar, Palar, Vaigai**.\n• **West-flowing** (into the Arabian Sea): **Sharavati, Mandovi, Periyar**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":182},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch4-178","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Namami Gange Programme","content":"• An **integrated conservation mission** launched by the Government of India in **2014**.\n• **Aim:** reduce pollution in the **River Ganga** and conserve and rejuvenate it.\n• **Long-term goal:** restore the river's endangered **biodiversity**; areas of high biodiversity are targeted for conservation.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":183},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-180","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Height above sea level and climate","content":"Temperature **decreases with height**. That is why hill stations such as **Udhagamandalam (Ooty)** and **Kodaikanal** in the Western Ghats are cooler than the nearby plains.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":184},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-181","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Kalbaisakhi","content":"In **Assam and West Bengal** pre-monsoon thunderstorms are called **Kalbaisakhi** — \"calamity of the month of Baisakh (April)\" — because they sometimes damage trees and property.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":185},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-182","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Cold dry season (winter) in India","content":"**Temperature**\n• North-west India below **10 °C**; about **21 °C** elsewhere in the plains; **Chennai about 24 °C**.\n• **Snowfall** at high places like Srinagar and Shimla; **cold waves** in the north.\n\n**Rainfall**\n• Scanty — a **high-pressure** area over land; winds blow from land to sea (dry).\n• Two areas still get some rain: the **Coromandel Coast** (Tamil Nadu — from the **north-east monsoon**) and **north-west India**.\n• The north winds are turned right by the **Coriolis force** and blow as north-east monsoons.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":186},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-183","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Advancing and retreating monsoon","content":"| | **Advancing monsoon** | **Retreating monsoon** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Direction | sea → land (south-west to north-east) | land → sea |\n| Winds | moist | dry |\n| Period | **June – September** | **October – November** |\n| Rain | **maximum** rainfall in India | less rain; the **east coast of south India** gets rain |\n\n**Relief effects:** the **Western Ghats** get heavy rain on the windward side; the **Deccan Plateau** (leeward) gets very little. The **Aravallis** lie parallel to the winds so **Rajasthan** is a desert (under 25 cm). The **Bay of Bengal branch** brings heavy rain to the north-east.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":187},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-184","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Tropical evergreen rainforests of India","content":"• Trees form **distinct layers**; thick undergrowth makes **commercial use difficult**.\n• **Species:** shisham, ebony, rosewood, mahogany, teak, gurjan, red cedar, bamboo.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":188},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-185","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Monsoon deciduous forests: economic importance","content":"Trees of great **economic importance**: **teak, sal, mahua, khair, sandalwood, jackfruit, semal, myrobalan and banyan**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":189},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-186","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Thorn and desert forests","content":"• Vegetation is **sparse**; stunted, thorny trees and bushes with **long roots** to reach groundwater and **modified stems to store water**.\n• **Species:** date palm, acacia, palas, cachi, kikar, babool.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":190},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-187","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Tidal (mangrove) forests","content":"• Trees have **stilt-like roots** that take in oxygen and **glossy evergreen leaves**.\n• **Species:** sundari, gorjan, lintal, casuarina, golpata, hogla (elephant grass), hetal and reeds.\n• Also called **mangroves**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":191},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-188","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Mountain (alpine) forests","content":"• **Grasses** on very high slopes; **coniferous** (softwood evergreen) forests below.\n• **Species:** sal, oak, ash, beech, chir pine, silver fir, chestnut, cedar, deodar.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":192},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-189","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Wildlife of India","content":"• **Asiatic lion** — only in the **Gir forest, Gujarat**; also panther, cheetah, snow leopard.\n• **Nilgai** (blue bull) north of the Godavari.\n• **Deer with 12-tipped horns:** swamp deer (Assam), barasingha (M.P.), **hangul (Kashmir)**.\n• **Red panda and Himalayan black bear** in snow-covered Himalayas.\n• **One-horned rhinoceros** — Assam and West Bengal (recently introduced in U.P.).\n• **Reptiles:** **gharial** (Gangetic crocodile) in Odisha, Assam, U.P. along large rivers; **saltwater crocodile** in the Sundarbans, Odisha, Andaman & Nicobar.\n• **Birds:** swallow, myna, owl, kite, parrot, pigeon, dove, crow, sparrow, bulbul; **national bird: peacock**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":193},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-190","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Direct uses of forests","content":"Forests give **timber** for construction, furniture, toys, railway sleepers, sports goods and wood carving, and **softwood, bamboo and grass** for pulp, paper and plywood.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":194},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-191","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Wildlife sanctuary: what is allowed","content":"A **sanctuary** is a protected area where wild animals live in their natural surroundings.\n• **Prohibited:** hunting, entering with weapons, lighting fires, using substances harmful to wildlife.\n• **May be allowed (controlled):** fishing and grazing of livestock.\n• The **Chief Wildlife Warden** may grant permission to enter for photography, research or tourism.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":195},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-192","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Vana Mahotsava (tree-planting festival)","content":"**Vana Mahotsava** is a tree-planting ceremony started in India to **maintain ecological balance** and prevent damage to the ozone layer. Schools and communities plant saplings and spread awareness of afforestation.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":196},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch7-193","topicId":"geo-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Zones of a biosphere reserve","content":"| Zone | What is allowed |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Core zone** | legally protected; no human disturbance; conserves biodiversity; only research that does not harm wildlife |\n| **Buffer zone** | surrounds the core; environmental research and recreation that do not harm wildlife |\n| **Transition zone** | human activities such as farming, grazing and those of indigenous people are permitted |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":197},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch8-196","topicId":"geo-ch8","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Meaning of human resources","content":"**Human resources** = the whole **population** of a country together with its **knowledge, skills, abilities, efficiency, far-sightedness and physical and mental capacity** to work.\n\n• Development needs the proper use of **natural and human resources**.\n• People are the **vital instruments of production**, so a country's progress depends on the **quality and efficiency of its people**.\n• Hence the need for **manpower planning** and better living conditions.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":198},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch8-197","topicId":"geo-ch8","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Skilled human resources and development","content":"**Skilled, knowledgeable people**\n• use resources rationally and **raise output and income**;\n• use modern technology; produce better goods and services.\n\n**Human resource development** changes attitudes, improves health, knowledge and skills, encourages **entrepreneurship, risk-taking and research**.\n\n**Education:** literacy and elementary, technical, vocational and professional education with skill training increase **employment opportunities** and incomes.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":199},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch8-198","topicId":"geo-ch8","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Skill training in India","content":"In India, **skill training has not received the attention it deserves**, except for limited professions such as engineers, doctors and technical experts like computer professionals.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":200},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch8-199","topicId":"geo-ch8","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Knowledge of India: skill and start-up schemes","content":"• **Startup India** and **Stand-up India** — for would-be entrepreneurs.\n• **National Skill Development Mission (2015)** — to give young people skills for **employment and productivity**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":201},{"id":"sup2-geo-ch8-200","topicId":"geo-ch8","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Goal 3: Ayushman Bharat","content":"**Ayushman Bharat** — a health-protection scheme covering **over 10 crore poor and vulnerable families**, with hospitalisation cover of up to **₹5 lakh per family per year**.\n\n**Benefits:** quality healthcare, timely treatment, higher productivity and efficiency, job creation, better quality of life.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":202},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch1-203","topicId":"hist-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Humanism — the heart of the Renaissance","content":"• In the **Middle Ages** everything revolved around religion; people were taught to focus on God, not on the world.\n• Renaissance thinkers believed **human life in this world was important**. They were called **Humanists**.\n• They shifted attention from \"godly affairs\" to **human affairs**.\n• **Francesco Petrarca (Petrarch)** is called the **Father of Humanism**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":203},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch1-204","topicId":"hist-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Impact of the Renaissance: art, literature and sculpture","content":"**Art**\n• The city of **Florence** produced great artists — **Leonardo da Vinci, Michelangelo, Raphael**.\n• Early artists painted **religious themes** (walls of churches and palaces covered with Bible scenes).\n• Later artists painted **secular themes** and made pictures more **realistic** by studying the human body — anatomy, expressions and movement.\n\n**Sculpture:** sculptors captured the grace of the human body — Michelangelo's **Pietà** in Rome is a famous example.\n\n**Literature — important writers and works**\n\n| Writer | Major work |\n| --- | --- |\n| Dante | The Divine Comedy |\n| Machiavelli | The Prince (*Il Principe*) |\n| Erasmus | The Praise of Folly |\n| William Shakespeare | Julius Caesar, The Merchant of Venice, Macbeth |\n| John Milton | Paradise Lost |\n| Geoffrey Chaucer | The Canterbury Tales |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":204},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch1-205","topicId":"hist-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Age of Discovery: why Europeans set out","content":"• The Renaissance created a **spirit of adventure** in Europe.\n• After the **fall of Constantinople (1453)** the Turks controlled the sea and land routes to the East, so Europeans needed **new routes**.\n• **Explorers** sought **fame, glory and wealth** and faced great hardships.\n• They reached the lands east of Europe — **India and China** — and the **New World (America)**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":205},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch1-206","topicId":"hist-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Inca and Aztec empires","content":"**Inca Empire**\n• Covered present-day **Ecuador, Chile, Peru, Argentina and Bolivia**.\n• The **largest empire in the world** in the early 16th century.\n• Known for great architecture, **terraced farming** and mountaintop settlements — the most famous being **Machu Picchu**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":206},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch1-207","topicId":"hist-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Economic impact of the Reformation","content":"With the **decline of feudalism**, new economic activities began — **banking, financing and new ways of trading** — leading to economic growth.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":207},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch1-208","topicId":"hist-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Why the Industrial Revolution began in Britain","content":"**Stable government:** Britain's stable government meant steady administration.\n• Traders and manufacturers worked freely, produced goods in bulk and sold them abroad at **huge profits**.\n• These profits were **invested in machinery** for still more production.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":208},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch1-209","topicId":"hist-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Impact of imperialism: positive and negative","content":"**Example:** the **Maldives** was a British protectorate from **1887 to 1965**; the British wanted it because it lay on the **trade routes** from Europe and Africa to the East.\n\n| Positive impact | Negative impact |\n| --- | --- |\n| More opportunities for Christian missionaries | Colonies became cheap sources of **raw materials** |\n| Spread of **Western education** | Industrial powers **exploited** colonies economically |\n| New technology: weapons, medicines, farming methods | Colonists **monopolised trade**; welfare of colonies was not a priority |\n| People learnt about new forms of government and organising | Competition for colonies led to **world wars** |\n| Led to **nationalism** and struggles for independence | Mass destruction and, finally, the end of imperial powers |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":209},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch2-210","topicId":"hist-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ The Boston Tea Party and the modern Tea Party movement","content":"• The modern **Tea Party movement** in the USA (a protest against high taxes and government debt) takes its name from the **Boston Tea Party of 1773**.\n• Anti-tax protesters often refer to the original event for inspiration.\n• In **2001**, some activists mailed tea bags to legislators as a symbolic protest.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":210},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch2-211","topicId":"hist-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Napoleon Bonaparte: a short life sketch","content":"• Born in **1769** on the island of **Corsica**.\n• Became a lieutenant in the French army in **1785**.\n• Gained the confidence of people and seniors through his **energy and quick decisions**, rose through the ranks.\n• Through his military exploits and efficiency he became **Napoleon I, Emperor of France**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":211},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch3-212","topicId":"hist-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ The growing anti-slavery movement","content":"• In the **19th century**, anti-slavery movements in America demanded **abolition**.\n• Southern masters were often cruel to slaves.\n• Sympathy for **abolitionists** grew, especially after **Harriet Beecher Stowe's** novel ***Uncle Tom's Cabin* (1852)** exposed the evils of slavery.\n• It changed northern opinion and helped bring on the **Civil War**.\n• Faced with abolition, the **southern states threatened to secede** (break away) from the Union.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":212},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch3-213","topicId":"hist-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Lincoln: the Great Emancipator","content":"• Lincoln's **Proclamation of Emancipation** declared slaves in the (rebel) southern states free — earning him the title **\"Great Emancipator\"**.\n• He wanted a liberal plan to **reconstruct the South**, but after his **assassination in 1865** military rule was set up.\n• Reconstruction of the southern states **ended officially in 1876**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":213},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch3-214","topicId":"hist-ch3","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Lincoln's Gettysburg Address (extract)","content":"> \"Four score and seven years ago our fathers brought forth on this continent, a new nation, conceived in Liberty, and dedicated to the proposition that all men are created equal… that government of the people, by the people, for the people, shall not perish from the earth.\"\n\n**Meaning:** Lincoln reminds Americans that the nation was founded on **liberty and equality** and that the war was fought to preserve **democracy**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":214},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch4-215","topicId":"hist-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Was Aurangzeb alone responsible for the Mughal decline?","content":"Many historians blame Aurangzeb, but the empire **lasted about 150 years after his death**. So he cannot be the only cause — several **other factors** (see the next note) also contributed.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":215},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch4-216","topicId":"hist-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Causes of the decline of the Mughal Empire","content":"**1. Wars of succession** — fights among claimants drained the empire's resources and weakened it.\n\n**2. Politics at the court** — weak later Mughals meant rivalry between four noble groups:\n• **Iranis** (from Persia)\n• **Turanis** (from Transoxiana)\n• **Afghans** (from the mountainous border regions beyond the Indus)\n• **Hindustanis**\nTheir power struggle and jealousies damaged administration.\n\n**3. Failure of the mansabdari system** — introduced by Akbar and efficient under him; after him the number of **mansabdars grew** but there were **not enough jagirs**, leading to a scramble and a weaker army (mansabdars did not keep the required troops).\n\n**4. Lack of military reforms** — the army kept **old equipment and tactics**; the Mughals had **no navy**, while the Europeans used strong navies.\n\n**5. Jagirdari crisis** — jagirs (land grants) were given in return for service; competition for good land grew and emperors ended up giving away **crown lands**, reducing their own income.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":216},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch4-217","topicId":"hist-ch4","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Key words: guerrilla warfare and Transoxiana","content":"• **Guerrilla warfare:** irregular fighting in which a small group uses ambushes, raids, surprise and mobility against a larger, less mobile army.\n• **Transoxiana:** a historical region of Central Asia, east of the Amu Darya and west of the Syr Darya rivers.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":217},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch2-218","topicId":"hist-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ India at the Paralympic Games (enrichment)","content":"• The Paralympics is a top international sporting event for people with disabilities.\n• Indian athletes have taken part in **eleven** Paralympic Games since **1960**, winning 31 medals (9 gold, 12 silver, 10 bronze).\n• **Murlikant Petkar**, a veteran of the 1965 Indo-Pak war who lost his arm, won India's **first Paralympic gold** — 50 m freestyle swimming at Heidelberg, 1972.\n• **Joginder Singh Bedi** won silver in shot put and medals in javelin and discus in 1984.\n• India's best-ever Paralympic performance came at **Tokyo 2020**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":218},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch5-222","topicId":"hist-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ The Carnatic Wars (1746–1763)","content":"• The **French** were the East India Company's biggest rival in India.\n• **Three Carnatic Wars** were fought between **1746 and 1763**.\n• The English East India Company defeated the French, captured their strongholds and **strengthened its control over southern India**.\n• **Carnatic coast:** the region of south India between the **Eastern Ghats and the Coromandel Coast** — today's Tamil Nadu, south-east Karnataka and southern Andhra Pradesh.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":219},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch5-223","topicId":"hist-ch5","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Anglo-French rivalry in India","content":"• By the 18th century the **Dutch and Portuguese** had lost most of their trading posts in India.\n• **Britain and France** emerged as the two main European powers.\n• Their competition for **trade monopoly and colonial possessions** led to the **Carnatic Wars**.\n• Many **Indian rulers** joined in, seeking British or French help in their own wars of succession.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":220},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch7-224","topicId":"hist-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Symbol wars: Tipu's tiger","content":"• **Tipu Sultan** identified himself with the **tiger**, a symbol of his kingdom's might.\n• He had a **mechanical tiger** made that showed a tiger overpowering a British soldier.\n• The British replied with a **medal** showing a **lion (Britain) conquering Tipu's tiger**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":221},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch6-225","topicId":"hist-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ The Battle of Plassey: how Siraj-ud-Daulah was defeated","content":"• A large part of the Nawab's army under **Mir Jafar did not fight**.\n• The Nawab left the field after the death of his commander **Mir Madan**; the disheartened army was easily defeated.\n• **Siraj-ud-Daulah** was captured and put to death.\n• **Mir Jafar** was made Nawab — a **puppet** — giving the English control of Bengal.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":222},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch6-226","topicId":"hist-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Jagat Seth: the banker behind the conspiracy","content":"• **Jagat Seth** was a wealthy Marwari banker of Murshidabad in Siraj-ud-Daulah's time.\n• Company historian Robert Orme called him \"the greatest banker in the known world\".\n• He **conspired with Mir Jafar and Robert Clive** against the Nawab.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":223},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch6-227","topicId":"hist-ch6","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Effects of the dual government: the Great Famine of 1770","content":"• The British cared only about **amassing wealth**, not about the welfare of the people.\n• When the **Great Famine of 1770** struck Bengal, **more than one-third of the population died**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":224},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch7-229","topicId":"hist-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Second Anglo-Mysore War (1780–84): the end","content":"• Hyder Ali was defeated at **Porto Novo**, but the war continued under his son **Tipu Sultan**.\n• It ended with the **Treaty of Mangalore (1784)**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":225},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch7-230","topicId":"hist-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ First Anglo-Maratha War (1775–82)","content":"• **Raghunath Rao (Raghoba)** asked the British for help; they agreed in return for **Salsette and Bassein** (important ports).\n• The infant **Madhav Rao II** was guided by **Nana Phadnavis**, leader of the Marathas, who defeated the British forces.\n• The war dragged on without a clear result and ended with the **Treaty of Salbai (1782)**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":226},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch7-231","topicId":"hist-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Third Anglo-Maratha War (1817–18)","content":"• Despite the **Subsidiary Alliance**, **Peshwa Baji Rao II** wanted to free himself from British control.\n• In **1817**, backed by the Holkars and Bhonsles, he attacked the **British Residency at Kirkee** (now Khadki, Maharashtra).\n• The Marathas were **completely defeated**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":227},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch7-232","topicId":"hist-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Anglo-Sikh relations and the Treaty of Amritsar (1809)","content":"• The British knew that controlling **Punjab** was essential for their empire in the subcontinent.\n• **Maharaja Ranjit Singh's** growing power worried the Company.\n• The **Treaty of Amritsar (1809)** made the **River Sutlej** the boundary between Sikh and British territory.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":228},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch7-233","topicId":"hist-ch7","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ First Anglo-Sikh War (1845–46)","content":"• The Sikh army **crossed the Sutlej**, violating the Treaty of Amritsar; Governor-General **Lord Hardinge** attacked.\n• The Sikhs were defeated — partly due to the **treachery of their own men**.\n• The war ended with the **Treaty of Lahore (1846)**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":229},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch8-234","topicId":"hist-ch8","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Connecting past and present: the Indian postal system","content":"• India today has **more than 1.5 lakh post offices**.\n• The government is modernising transport and communication while keeping them **affordable**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":230},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch1-235","topicId":"hist-ch8","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Recording India's flora and fauna in the colonial period","content":"In the colonial period the **Dutch, French and British** collected information about India's plants and animals and asked artists for detailed sketches. Many Europeans and their wives also sketched and painted as a hobby, so India's flora and fauna were well documented.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":231},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch9-237","topicId":"hist-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Socio-religious causes of the 1857 uprising: Western education","content":"Sections of Indian society **objected to the importance given to Western education** at the cost of **traditional learning**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":232},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch9-238","topicId":"hist-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Immediate cause of 1857: the greased cartridges","content":"Rumours spread in the sepoy camps that the **grease on the new cartridges contained fat of pigs and cows**.\n• Hindu sepoys (who revere the cow) and Muslim sepoys (who avoid the pig) both **refused** to use them.\n• This became the spark that set off the **Revolt of 1857**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":233},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch9-239","topicId":"hist-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Spread of the revolt from Delhi","content":"News of the events at **Delhi** spread rapidly and provoked uprisings among sepoys in many districts, including the **North-Western Provinces**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":234},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch9-240","topicId":"hist-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Primary source: British soldier's account of the recapture of Delhi","content":"A British soldier's letter describes that, when the British entered Delhi, **everyone found inside the walls was bayoneted on the spot** — including ordinary residents hiding in their houses, not mutineers.\n\n**What it shows:** the **brutal revenge** taken by the British after the revolt, even on civilians.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":235},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch7-241","topicId":"hist-ch9","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Key word: the Uprising of 1857","content":"**Uprising of 1857:** a revolt in India that was the **culmination of decades of resentment** against the exploitation, oppression and injustice of British rule.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":236},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch10-242","topicId":"hist-ch10","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Iconic social-religious reformers","content":"The best-known reformers of 19th-century India:\n• **Raja Ram Mohan Roy**\n• **Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar**\n• **Swami Vivekananda**","source":"ocr_supplement","order":237},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch10-243","topicId":"hist-ch10","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Why reform movements began","content":"Many **British administrators and thinkers criticised** traditional practices and attitudes towards women in Indian society. This criticism made Indians **look critically at their own society** and start a series of **reform movements**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":238},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch10-244","topicId":"hist-ch10","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Socio-religious reform movements: introduction","content":"• In the 19th century Indian reformers advocated **deliberate change** in traditional social, cultural and religious attitudes and customs to remove narrow practices.\n• They were the leaders of the social and cultural **renaissance** of the 19th century.\n• The renaissance **began in Bengal**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":239},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch10-245","topicId":"hist-ch10","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Reform movements in western India","content":"Western India also saw social reform movements. The most notable were:\n• **Prarthana Samaj**\n• **Arya Samaj**","source":"ocr_supplement","order":240},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch10-246","topicId":"hist-ch10","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Primary source: the plan for the MAO College","content":"Syed Ahmad Khan's plan for the **Muhammadan Anglo-Oriental (MAO) College** at Aligarh:\n• To make it a university like **Oxford or Cambridge**, with a mosque attached.\n• Resident boys must join the five daily prayers; students of other religions are exempt.\n• Abusive language, smoking and betel chewing are banned.\n• **No corporal punishment** that hurts a student's self-respect.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":241},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch10-247","topicId":"hist-ch10","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Syed Ahmad Khan and the Aligarh Movement","content":"• **Syed Ahmad Khan** — Muslim educator, jurist and author; a progressive realist.\n• The main force behind the **revival of the Indian Muslims** in the late 19th century.\n• He started the **Aligarh Movement** (modern, scientific education for Muslims).","source":"ocr_supplement","order":242},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch10-248","topicId":"hist-ch10","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Literature, art and theatre in the reform period","content":"**Literature**\n• Educated Indians wrote poems, stories and essays in newspapers, magazines and books — in English **and regional languages**.\n• Famous writers: **Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay, Sarojini Naidu, Tarabai Shinde, Subramania Bharati**.\n\n**Art and theatre**\n• **Abanindranath Tagore** and **Raja Ravi Varma** blended Indian traditions with European painting techniques.\n\nThe 19th century was thus a period of **social and cultural revival**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":243},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch11-249","topicId":"hist-ch11","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ What is nationalism?","content":"**Nationalism** is a deep love for one's motherland and the desire to free it from foreign rule.\n\nThe end of the 19th and the start of the 20th century saw nationalist feelings rise among Indians, which led to the **Indian national movement**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":244},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch11-250","topicId":"hist-ch11","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Role of the press and literature","content":"Nationalist literature — **Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay's *Anandamath*** and **Rabindranath Tagore's *Ghare Baire*** — awakened people culturally, taught them about their heritage and **aroused nationalist aspirations**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":245},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch11-251","topicId":"hist-ch11","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Rediscovering India's past","content":"Indians rediscovered the **richness of Vedic philosophy**, the wisdom of rulers such as **Chandragupta Maurya**, and the glory of the **Gupta** and **Mughal** empires.\n\nThis gave them **pride and self-confidence** to demand freedom.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":246},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch11-252","topicId":"hist-ch11","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Lord Lytton's Delhi Durbar (1877)","content":"• In **1877** an Imperial Durbar was held in Delhi with great show to proclaim **Queen Victoria Empress of India**.\n• It was held while **south India was suffering a famine** and the British offered no relief.\n• Such policies **angered Indians** and pushed them towards organised action against the British.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":247},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch12-253","topicId":"hist-ch12","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Moderates: criticism and achievements","content":"**Criticism:** they achieved few of their objectives and were called a **\"microscopic minority\"** — leaders of a small section while the masses were unaffected.\n\n**Even so, they achieved:** political awakening, an **economic critique of British rule**, a base for the later national movement and a common platform for Indians (see the achievements listed in the chapter).","source":"ocr_supplement","order":248},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch12-254","topicId":"hist-ch12","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Partition of Bengal (1905)","content":"• **Lord Curzon**, the Viceroy, partitioned Bengal.\n• **Official reason:** Bengal was too large to administer well.\n• **Real reason:** to **divide Hindus and Muslims** and weaken the national movement.\n• Bengal was formally divided on **16 October 1905** into **East Bengal and West Bengal**.\n• It strengthened the radicals' belief in the oppressive nature of British rule and triggered the **Swadeshi and Boycott movements**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":249},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch1-255","topicId":"hist-ch12","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ After the partition: Dhaka","content":"Although the partition of Bengal was **annulled in 1911**, **Dhaka** later became the capital of **East Pakistan** after the Partition of India in 1947, and is today the capital of **independent Bangladesh**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":250},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch12-256","topicId":"hist-ch12","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ The Boycott Movement and government repression","content":"**Boycott Movement**\n• People were asked to **abstain from buying British or foreign goods**.\n• They were also asked **not to send children to British schools and colleges**.\n\n**Government response**\n• Harsh repression — revolutionaries were tracked down and captured and hideouts destroyed.\n• Some, like **Khudiram Bose**, were **hanged**; others, like **Barin Ghosh**, were imprisoned.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":251},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch12-257","topicId":"hist-ch12","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Russo-Japanese War (1904–05)","content":"• A conflict between **Russia and Japan** over supremacy in **Manchuria and Korea**.\n• Fought mainly on the **Liaodong Peninsula**, at **Mukden (now Shenyang)** and in the **Yellow Sea**.\n• **Japan won** and became the **first Asian power in modern times to defeat a European power** — inspiring nationalists across Asia, including India.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":252},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch12-258","topicId":"hist-ch12","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Muslim League's change of aim (1916)","content":"• In **1916** the Muslim League changed its aim from loyalty to the British to **self-government within the British Empire**.\n• Muslims were upset by British support for abolishing the **Ottoman Empire** — the Sultan was also the **caliph**, the religious head of Muslims worldwide.\n• They saw this as **disrespect to their religious sentiments**, and their faith in the British government was shaken.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":253},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch12-259","topicId":"hist-ch12","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Key word: boycott","content":"**Boycott:** refusing to buy British or foreign goods and refusing to attend British schools and colleges.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":254},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch11-260","topicId":"hist-ch11","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Pandita Ramabai at the Congress (1889)","content":"**Pandita Ramabai** was one of the **10 women delegates** registered at the Congress session of **1889** — a pioneer in the cause of women's education and widows' welfare.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":255},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch13-262","topicId":"hist-ch13","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ The concept of Satyagraha","content":"A **satyagrahi**:\n• firmly believes in **truth and non-violence**;\n• **resists evil** without violence;\n• is **fearless and strong**;\n• is ready to **sacrifice and suffer**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":256},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch13-263","topicId":"hist-ch13","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Early satyagrahas win over national leaders","content":"Gandhi's early experiments in satyagraha won over popular leaders: in **Bihar, Dr Rajendra Prasad** and in **Gujarat, Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel** became enthusiastic, staunch supporters of his methods.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":257},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch13-264","topicId":"hist-ch13","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Chauri Chaura (1922) and the end of Non-Cooperation","content":"• At the peak of the Non-Cooperation Movement, violence erupted at **Chauri Chaura** (Gorakhpur district, Uttar Pradesh) in **1922**.\n• Police fired on a peaceful procession; the angry mob **burnt the police station**, killing some policemen.\n• Gandhi disapproved of the violence and **immediately called off the movement**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":258},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch13-265","topicId":"hist-ch13","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Boycott of the Simon Commission","content":"• Indians boycotted the **Simon Commission** because it was an **all-white commission with no Indian member**.\n• Its recommendations were also **unacceptable** to national leaders.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":259},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch13-266","topicId":"hist-ch13","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Salt March and the Civil Disobedience Movement","content":"• At **Dandi**, Gandhi picked up a handful of salt to **break the salt law**; the act was copied all over India.\n• The **Civil Disobedience Movement** became an **all-India mass movement**: strikes, hartals, boycott of offices, schools and colleges.\n• People refused to pay taxes or buy foreign goods; the movement was **non-violent** and **women took part**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":260},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch13-267","topicId":"hist-ch13","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Government repression","content":"Hundreds were injured or killed in **lathi-charges and police firing**. Nationalist feeling was strong, and the British realised that **real reforms** were needed to pacify Indians.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":261},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch13-268","topicId":"hist-ch13","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Gandhi–Irwin Pact (1931)","content":"| Gandhi agreed to | Lord Irwin agreed to |\n| --- | --- |\n| **suspend** the Civil Disobedience Movement | **release all prisoners** |\n| attend the **Second Round Table Conference** | **allow people to make salt** |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":262},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch13-269","topicId":"hist-ch13","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Suppression of the Quit India Movement","content":"During the 1942 movement, people **cut telegraph lines and damaged railway tracks**. The government called out the **army**; **thousands were killed** and the movement was suppressed.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":263},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch13-270","topicId":"hist-ch13","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ The Indian National Army (INA)","content":"• **September 1942:** **Captain Mohan Singh** gathered 40,000 Indian soldiers to form the **Azad Hind Fauj (INA)**.\n• **Subhas Chandra Bose** took supreme command; in **July 1943** he took the INA's salute and was hailed as **Netaji** (\"the leader\").\n• The INA made a heroic contribution to the freedom struggle and Bose became an **icon of patriotism**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":264},{"id":"sup2-hist-ch13-271","topicId":"hist-ch13","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Key words: salt laws","content":"**Salt laws:** British laws that made **production and possession of salt by Indians illegal** — only the government could make and collect salt at its depots.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":265},{"id":"sup2-civ-ch1-272","topicId":"civ-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Rajya Sabha: qualification for membership","content":"• Qualifications and disqualifications are the **same as for the Lok Sabha**.\n• But a candidate for the Rajya Sabha must be at least **30 years old** (Lok Sabha: 25).","source":"ocr_supplement","order":266},{"id":"sup2-civ-ch1-273","topicId":"civ-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ The executive: who enforces the laws","content":"The legislature **makes** laws; the **executive** **enforces** them.\n\n| Level | Executive |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Union** | President (head), Vice-President, Prime Minister and Council of Ministers |\n| **State** | Governor (head), Chief Minister and Council of Ministers |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":267},{"id":"sup2-civ-ch1-274","topicId":"civ-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Impeachment of the President","content":"**Impeachment** is the procedure laid down in the Constitution by which **Parliament can remove the President**.\n\nThe President can be impeached for:\n• **violating the Constitution**, or\n• being guilty of **treason or corruption**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":268},{"id":"sup2-civ-ch1-275","topicId":"civ-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Appointment of judges","content":"| Post | Appointed by | In consultation with |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Chief Justice of the Supreme Court** | the President | judges of the Supreme Court and High Courts |\n| **Other Supreme Court judges** | the President | the Chief Justice and other judges of the Supreme Court and High Courts |\n| **Chief Justice of a High Court** | the President | the Chief Justice of India and the Governor of the state |\n| **Other High Court judges** | the President | the same, and also the Chief Justice of that High Court |","source":"ocr_supplement","order":269},{"id":"sup2-civ-ch1-276","topicId":"civ-ch1","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Writs","content":"The **Supreme Court and High Courts** can issue **writs** to enforce the **Fundamental Rights**.\n\nA **writ** is a written command from a court telling someone to act or to stop acting illegally.\n\n**Five writs:** habeas corpus, mandamus, prohibition, quo warranto and certiorari.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":270},{"id":"sup2-civ-ch2-277","topicId":"civ-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Why the League of Nations was set up","content":"• **World War I** (1914–18) caused death, injury and destruction on an unimaginable scale.\n• People everywhere wanted **peace**.\n• The **League of Nations** was founded for this purpose; its first session was in **1919**.\n• It was the forerunner of the United Nations.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":271},{"id":"sup2-civ-ch2-278","topicId":"civ-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ The Atlantic Charter","content":"• **14 August 1941:** British PM **Winston Churchill** and US President **Franklin Roosevelt** met on a battleship and signed the **Atlantic Charter**.\n• Its proposals became the basis for the **United Nations**.\n• **January 1942:** 26 countries signed the **Declaration by United Nations**.\n• **25 April 1945:** the United Nations Conference on International Organisation began in San Francisco; the UN came into existence on **24 October 1945**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":272},{"id":"sup2-civ-ch2-279","topicId":"civ-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ UN peacekeeping forces","content":"The UN has **peacekeeping forces** made up of personnel from member states.\n\nThey are sent to regions where armed conflict has **recently ended**, to discourage the fighters from starting war again.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":273},{"id":"sup2-civ-ch2-280","topicId":"civ-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Why the UN is needed today","content":"The world needs organisations like the UN more than ever because of the **threat of terrorism** and **communal hatred and discord**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":274},{"id":"sup2-civ-ch2-281","topicId":"civ-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ UNESCO: mission","content":"**UNESCO** works for **peace and security** by promoting cooperation among nations through **education, science and culture**.\n\nIts belief: the best way to prevent war is to **educate people about the benefits of peace**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":275},{"id":"sup2-civ-ch1-282","topicId":"civ-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Child labour: a social evil","content":"**Child labour** is a social evil that India has long struggled with.\n\n• Children are often employed in **sweatshops, factories, construction sites and homes**.\n• **UNICEF** and the **ILO** work against child labour and for children's education, health and protection.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":276},{"id":"sup2-civ-ch2-283","topicId":"civ-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ World Trade Organization (WTO)","content":"The **WTO** oversees **international trade** and helps countries with exports through the **International Trade Centre**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":277},{"id":"sup2-civ-ch2-284","topicId":"civ-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Key word: sweatshop","content":"**Sweatshop:** a factory or workshop (especially in the clothing industry) where workers are employed for **very low wages, long hours and in poor conditions**.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":278},{"id":"sup-x-phy-density","topicId":"ch2-measurement","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Table: densities of common substances","content":"| Solids / liquids | kg/m³ | g/cm³ | Substance | kg/m³ | g/cm³ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Gold | 19,300 | 19.3 | Mercury | 13,600 | 13.6 |\n| Lead | 11,300 | 11.3 | Water (4 °C) | 1,000 | 1.00 |\n| Silver | 10,500 | 10.5 | Sea water | 1,030 | 1.03 |\n| Copper | 8,900 | 8.9 | Milk | 1,030 | 1.03 |\n| Iron | 7,800 | 7.8 | Glycerine | 1,300 | 1.30 |\n| Zinc | 4,200 | 4.2 | Olive oil | 900 | 0.90 |\n| Aluminium | 2,700 | 2.7 | Turpentine oil | 850 | 0.85 |\n| Glass | 2,600 | 2.6 | Kerosene | 800 | 0.80 |\n| Marble | 2,500 | 2.5 | | | |\n| Concrete | 2,320 | 2.32 | | | |\n| Hard wood | 800 | 0.80 | | | |\n| Soft wood | 525 | 0.525 | | | |\n| Cork | 200 | 0.20 | | | |\n| Ice | 920 | 0.92 | | | |\n\n**Gases:** air 1.29 kg/m³; oxygen 1.43; steam 0.80; hydrogen 0.09; carbon dioxide 1.96 kg/m³ (that is, about 10⁻³ g/cm³).\n\n**Remember:** 1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³. A substance with density **less than water** (1000 kg/m³) floats on it.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":279},{"id":"sup-x-phy-refr","topicId":"ch5-light","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Table: refractive index of some media","content":"| Medium | Refractive index | Medium | Refractive index |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Air | 1.0003 | Glycerine | 1.48 |\n| Ice | 1.31 | Glass | 1.50 |\n| Water | 1.33 | Rock salt | 1.54 |\n| Alcohol | 1.36 | Ruby | 1.71 |\n| Kerosene | 1.44 | Sapphire | 1.77 |\n| Turpentine oil | 1.47 | Diamond | 2.42 |\n\nA **higher refractive index** means the medium is **optically denser** and bends light more.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":280},{"id":"sup-x-geo-density","topicId":"geo-ch2","type":"note","subtopic":"➕ Countries with the highest population density (2023)","content":"| Country | People per km² (approx.) |\n| --- | --- |\n| Singapore | about 8,700 |\n| Bahrain | 1,868 |\n| Maldives | 1,738 |\n| Bangladesh | 1,301 |\n| Holy See (Vatican City) | 1,162 |\n\n**Population density** = total population ÷ area. Despite its very high density, Singapore keeps overpopulation problems low through high living standards and careful planning.","source":"ocr_supplement","order":281}];
